{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1551265-thoughts-on-the-savage-age-intro-bundle","id":1551265,"title":"Thoughts on the Savage Age Intro Bundle","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2025-05-24T17:14:27-07:00","id":1551265,"title":"<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/icons/icon3.png\" width=\"16\" height=\"16\" alt=\"\" /> Thoughts on the Savage Age Intro Bundle","content":"<br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">So I recently picked up the Savage Age Intro Bundle and thought it would be useful to write down my thoughts as I went through the books in the bundle. Including the fillable savage age character sheet, the bundle includes 5 pdfs, Accounting for the Dead, and Tribebooks: Celican, Khara and Naghaluu. I am going to start with Accounting for the Dead since I think it will give a better sense of the overall feel of the setting. It will probably take several posts to get through each book as there is lots to cover.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Accounting for the Dead</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I like how the pages in this are laid out, the pages aren\u2019t too busy. I find with some Werewolf the Apocalypse books the desire to have cool backgrounds on pages can actually make it difficult to read at times.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Prologue: Caliah</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I like the first part about Fox declaring that they weren\u2019t ready.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I am not sure how I feel about the second part about the Mokole. First it seems confusing the way it talks about finding some of the genocided Fera, but I think it is referring to their spirits not actually members of those groups. Also it seems like something that would have earned them considerable punishment by a very powerful spirit, possibly even Gaia herself. After all it certainly seems to be implied by the collective write ups of the War of Rage that the Garou doomed everyone by wiping out the Apis and Grondr and eventually the Camazotz and nearly destroying the Gurahl. On the other hand, it is generally well written and isn\u2019t necessarily true and I think Werewolf is generally a better setting when all the Fera have baggage and reasons for internal conflict.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Welcome to the Savage Age</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I think this short part is very useful, far to many rpg books assume anyone reading them will have a bunch of knowledge about what the book is for and how to use it. I think it is worth a couple of pages to situate the book.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Khara</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I do like how there is a link to the tribebook if you had gotten Accounting for the Dead on its own. I like how their role is immediately tied to the role of the modern Baset and the immediate link to the ideas that knowledge is power, and power corrupts. I appreciate the editor\u2019s note about what is overlap with their tribebook and what is not.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I did find the mention of Braga-Khara and Dur-Khara confusing, I am sure their tribebook explains it, but this section could have used a very quick explanation. I think they are basically regional sub-tribes.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">This section does have a considerable number of gifts, twenty-three if I counted correctly. That is a lot of new options for a Khara PC which I think is really good.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I am not sure what the point of being able to take both Scent of the True Form and Sense Moon is. The two gifts appear to be different versions of the same ability and Sense Moon along with another level 5 gift requires you to have the old Mokole book which many people will not have. Also clarification would be good, is the Khara version of Scent of the True Form still optimized for finding Garou? I went to check W20 Changing Breeds and the Mokole seem to have replaced Sense Moon with Scent of the True Form, though they call it Sight of the True Form; that book also does not clarify. If I was running a campaign I would make it clear that the Khara version is optimized for Khara/Baset not Garou.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Personally I think it would be better when referencing gifts from other places to always make clear exactly what list the gift is drawn from saying Garou when it is a Philodox gift or Mokole when it is a specific Auspice is mildly confusing. And also needing the old Corax book for several gifts is not great, again I have access to that book, but many people newer to Werewolf won\u2019t have it.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I think some clarification on how Deny the Stalker and Whisper Catching interact would have been useful.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I particularly like Accursed Secret, there is lots of interesting stuff both a player and storyteller could do with it. Are the Khara responsible for the Yava of all the Baset tribes?</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Overall I find the gifts section of the Khara part of the pdf to be good despite the concerns I identified here. They all seem to fit within a few roles that the Khara perform and allow for some very different Khara characters to be developed. It is clear that the writers did their research as they pulled gifts from four different sourcebooks, but I think using non-W20 books was not the best choice; without access to those books you lose around 1/5 of the options presented.</span></span><br /> \u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12161-browman","name":"Browman","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"135"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-05-25T17:34:31-07:00","id":1551281,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Today I am looking at the next two sections of Accounting for the Dead, the Apis and the Grondr, both Fera that have a decent amount of information in W20 Changing Breeds.<br /> <br /> <b>The Apis</b></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The expanded Apis background is very interesting, I like how they in many ways show how the Triat is supposed to work. Their ability to shift from one animal kin to another is interesting and I learned about some very odd prehistoric creatures in the process. It does raise questions of how they weren\u2019t able to help other Fera make the same transition, which could potentially be some interesting plot hooks. Same with the idea of the rare mesonyx throwback birth, lots of things that could be used for.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The different regional groups of Apis is another good addition even if there isn\u2019t a ton of detail. I would have liked it if there was some indication of stat changes for non-aurox Apis. The modern nights ideas are interesting, including the idea of how a limited number of them could have survived, that page feels like it could have had more however or the previous part continued. There was more than half a page of extra space before getting into the more rules heavy stuff.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Apis gifts section is extensive with 20 general gifts that any Apis can take, and all of them are new. Some of them seem pretty powerful, though in line with the type of stuff Apis should have gifts for. There are another 15 Auspice gifts, 1 for each level for each of the 3 Auspices the Apis have. Again all of these are new. While I haven\u2019t looked at Tribebook Khara yet which may be entirely new gifts, it feels like a lot more work went into the Apis section of this book.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I have far less concerns with this section than the Khara equivalent. A few gifts are oddly phrased and some of them seem perhaps too powerful. While not a combat ability, Watch the Herd seems really, really good, even for a 4th level gift. 1 gnosis for full sensory sharing with permanent gnosis number of people for a month seems too much. Muddy the Pool should probably also have a resource cost to use it or require the Apis to be touching the creature or something. Stamp of Fury is also very powerful and doesn\u2019t actually say how long it lasts for, I assume it is a scene or until you have used all your successes.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Overall however the gifts section of the Apis portion of the book is excellent, lots of the gifts could easily generate plot hooks and with so many Apis unique gifts you can make characters that really feel different from others. Manifest the Chimerical Legacy is super cool, but use it in front of the wrong character and you could easily get declared to be some kind of tainted monster.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I am glad to see that thought was given to Apis rites, however the one non-minor rite can only be used annually at a specific time which doesn\u2019t seem that useful. It feels more like something that adds cool flavour to a scene players witness if they visited an Apis Fold at the appropriate time of year. While probably more of a background thing, description of a rite about shifting an Apis\u2019s breed to new animals could have been really cool.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Overall I like the Apis section, it is definitely better than the Khara section. I don\u2019t think the Khara section combined with W20 Changing Breeds gives enough information to have them as PCs in a campaign but the Apis I feel like I could.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Grondr</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I like the expanded Grondr lore, but it feels like it is very broad but not deep enough. Lots of stuff is mentioned but not covered in enough detail to be super useful. Like they tend to be solitary or in pairs, yet also sounders; but we don\u2019t really get an idea of how that works. I do really like the information about how the Grondr are some of the most likely Fera to fall to the Wyrm because of the filth they are constantly fighting. The bit about most of them preferring Neanderthals over Homo Sapiens is interesting and may help feed into their eventual extinction.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The section on relations with the other Fera is good, it gives a clear picture of how they fit into the wider group. And I like that it isn\u2019t the same type of relationship with each of the other Fera.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Adding in some different tribal/regional groups is interesting, but there isn\u2019t a ton of detail. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I like some aspects of the War of Rage section, but other parts feel odd. The write-up suggests the war started around the same time that humans showed up on the scene, but that would mean that the war between the Garou and the Grondr lasted for tens of thousands of years which seems silly. And the whole, \u201cthen whoever was left fell to the Wyrm en mass and become skullpigs\u201d seems very handwaved and simplified. Earlier it was suggested that the Grondr were already more likely to fall to the Wyrm, were those also skull pigs? Were some of those earlier fallen responsible for dooming the last uncorrupted members of their kind?</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Given what we know about the Siberakh it seems odd to see them mentioned here. Actually it feels odd to have any divisions of the Garou mentioned, since there is little context so far about any Garou organization in the Savage Age.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The ideas presented in the modern nights section are interesting, but from a formatting point of view, the last page leaves a lot of empty space. That space could have been very useful in some parts of the Grondr write-up expanding on something.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">There are 26 Grondr gifts listed, all of them are new; it really feels at this point like the Khara got the short end of the stick here. The lack of anything mechanically separating any of the tribes feels odd, as does no expansion to have breed specific gifts. I think it would have been more interesting to have most of the gifts be tribe and breed specific; since other than starting rage and a few specializations, there is little mechanical difference in options between any type of Grondr.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">That being said, I really like most of the gifts, lots of them are not combat oriented and add interesting options. There is a typo with Speak, referencing Apis instead of Grondr, clearly it was originally an Apis gift it is unclear if it is supposed to be a Grondr one or if it was put in the wrong place. Petrifying Countenance is the one I am concerned might be overpowered, yes it has a high resource cost, but it instantly kills the target.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I like the two rites, both feel logical though they are complicated and wordy. Given that the Grondr only have 1 existing rite, I would have liked a couple more. Earlier in the write-up extensive greeting rites to ensure the other Grondr aren\u2019t tainted are mentioned, that could have been a really cool and flavourful low level rite. There is probably space on the last rites page for another short rite.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Overall I like the Grondr section, perhaps not quite as much as the Apis section, but I feel like I have enough information combined with W20 Changing Breeds to use the Grondr in a Savage Age campaign.</span></span><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12161-browman","name":"Browman","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"135"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-05-26T18:03:05-07:00","id":1551306,"title":"","content":"<b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Anupu-Ba-El</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The name is a mouthful.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The initial fiction piece is good, it gives a good indication of the mindset of an average Anupu.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The lore write up is interesting but it feels off for the Garou to work with them. If anything I would think that the Garou would dislike a Fera so close to them even more. Anupu NPCs could be very useful for planting plot hooks or giving the party information. A more umbral focused campaign would definitely benefit from having an Anupu character. Mentioning the Kucha Ekundu here is interesting, the modern Garou don\u2019t know very much about them and all the sources I have read suggest that their numbers are small and have always been so. I would have thought they were a more recent thing than the time period Savage Age covers.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The section never directly says how they operate but it is strongly implied that they mostly operate independently.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The relations with other Fera section feels a bit too uniform in opinion. Fera frequently fail to understand the role of other types of Fera and judge them. It would have been good to see at least one Fera mentioned that the Anupu don\u2019t really get along with. The Khara seem like an easy option, from their point of view the Anupu could be getting secrets the Khara would rather stay hidden from spirits of the dead.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Ohk-Rahm are interesting, I hope they will get more detail at some point.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Their War of Rage section is interesting. The information about the Garou Theurges is very interesting. It would be cool to see a set of gifts for an \u201cold school Theurge\u201d with those hedge magics. I guess you could use some of the various hedge magic rules, but those don\u2019t always interact well with the rest of the Werewolf system.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Again some kind of information on how they shifted from jackal to wolves would be interesting, being able to shift your animal kin seems to definitely be more of a thing in Savage Age and some information about it would be cool, it seems like an interesting thread to build a campaign off of. I wish the modern nights section mentioned how the Ohk-Rahm could have survived.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Anupu gifts section has 25 general gifts half new, half taken from existing sources, mostly Garou but a few Nuwisha and one Kitsune. Re-using Garou gifts makes more sense for these guys than other Fera I think. Again the Khara seem to have gotten a raw deal. Pathfinder\u2019s Cord is a really cool ability that several other Fera would love to know, but it\u2019s rules are are unclear, I assume you have to spend 1 gnosis; but it would also be good to know how long it can last for, if some Anupu went into the Deep Umbra and was gone for weeks would one use last the whole time?</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Getting access to multiple rank 3 gifts at rank 1 seems very powerful even if they aren\u2019t combat related. Space is repeatedly used to either list Silent Strider gifts or even list out specifics of the gift only to note it is identical to the Silent Strider gift, this seems like a waste of space that could better be used for something else. Gate of the Moon I am willing to give a pass because the Anupu can use it in ways the Silent Striders can\u2019t.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">There are also 4 lupus breed gifts, half new, half re-used. Both the re-used abilities are gained at lower levels than the original source which I am not keen on.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Overall I like the Anupu gifts, they seem very focused in a specific direction, so if you were running campaign that didn\u2019t heavily involve spirits and the umbra an Anupu won\u2019t have much unique to bring to the table. I would have liked to see at least one clearly combat gift. I guess part of my worry is with the set of gifts they have, their usefulness will be very swingy, if the session is focused on umbra and spirit stuff or you need to get a message somewhere quickly, this character will probably completely outshine the rest of the party. But without those things, they aren\u2019t terribly useful.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I like both the Anupu rites, Descent into the Dusk especially offers some really cool story ideas especially if someone is using sentients to power the rite.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The two fetishes are interesting, but again a page less than half full, this easily could have had another two fetishes or another rite. It feels odd that the Anupu get two fetishes when it makes more sense for them to use Garou fetishes yet the Apis and Grondr don\u2019t get any and their W20 entries don\u2019t have any unique fetishes either.</span></span> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Neanderthals</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I do really like the inclusion of Neanderthals as separate from Homo Sapiens, I think that can add some really interesting and unique dynamics to a Savage Age game. I appreciate the quick rundown of who Neanderthals were, most people have nothing beyond the most basic understanding of how they relate to Homo Sapiens. All the various sections of this part are useful and the bits about Neanderthals and the Umbra help make them different from Homo Sapiens in a campaign.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">This section also includes 9 new gifts that Neaderthal breed Fera can take. All are interesting and several requiring cannibalistic activity create interesting potential story ideas as not everyone will be cool with such activity. I do wish they clarified the exact difficulty of the check with all the gifts.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">There are also 5 new fetishes and 3 new talens. Most of these are interesting, but adding a sex fetish doesn\u2019t seem super useful and may encourage some players in directions that not everyone is comfortable with in an rpg session. </span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Playing in the Savage Age</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Organizationally I feel like this section should have been the first part of the book, details of various Fera in the Savage Age are super cool but this section is the meat of the book. Reminding people of key aspects of this era is very helpful for situating how this Savage Age is different. Before reaching this section I didn\u2019t feel super confident running anything in Savage Age. I probably still only really feel comfortable running a campaign focused around either the Apis or Grondr with NPCs from other Fera, I don\u2019t think there is enough information in this book to run a Garou or other Fera game without significant work as the storyteller. Similarly I don\u2019t think there is enough about the Khara from W20 Changing breeds and this book to use them as the focus and the Anupu feel too focused to run a campaign centered around them.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Building a Character in the Savage Age</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Short but important and useful section. Formatting wise it seems odd to have it be 1.2 pages, that extra space could have been used for something, maybe a few \u201cQuick Build\u201d suggestions for attribute and ability spreads.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Savage Age Animals</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Super useful short section to have. A few odd formatting things in this section and the last page had space for another 2 or 3 animals.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Overall thoughts</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Despite what it may seem, I really like this book. My comments about various things that are odd or could use more information are from my want for the book to be great rather than just good. I think the Apis and Grondr parts are the best and most useful of the Fera sections and would be useful for any Apis or Grondr based campaign. Without having looked at the Khara tribebook (which is up next) I don\u2019t think there is enough information to use them as the focus of a campaign between this and W20 Changing Breeds. And the Anupu feel like a Fera that using more than one of as a PC in a campaign would be difficult. I wish this book had even like 1 page detailing the Garou briefly in this era, lots of stuff is suggested and hinted at in various places but it would take a lot of work by a storyteller to run a Garou focused game using only this book to get the Savage Age stuff from.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I should also note that the artwork in the book is consistently good.</span></span><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12161-browman","name":"Browman","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"135"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-05-27T19:24:23-07:00","id":1551320,"title":"","content":"<b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Tribebook: Khara</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Who doesn\u2019t love the idea of Were-sabrecats/tigers? I feel like if you don\u2019t I wouldn\u2019t want to game with you.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Prologue: Caliah</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">This immediately starts off with calling the storyteller both a Qualmi and a Khara which is odd. The short stories are good and really help establish the character of the Khara immediately. Before getting into anything else we already have a good feel for them and how several other Fera see them.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Chapter 1: Tribal Background</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The information presented here is good, like their distrust of writing things down in case someone else found it. And their nature making it difficult for even other Baset to want to work with them. Nearly everyone can understand how a smug, I know better than you type will cause problems. Formatting wise, this is not a large book, having a page that is \u2154 rds empty is less than ideal.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Chapter 2: On the Nature of Secrets</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Like I said in my thoughts on the Khara portion of Accounting for the Dead, I really like how they are leaning into the Knowledge is Power and Power Corrupts ideas. I like that there is an entire chapter diving into how this task of finding and keeping secrets, especially powerful or dangerous ones impacted them and how they operated. There is lots of stuff that can be useful here in building interesting and layered characters.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The idea of Khara conclaves is great. Such an event could be a really cool part of campaign, perhaps even one that doesn\u2019t centre around the Khara, for there are many Fera that would love to eavesdrop on such an event.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The idea that the Khara helped somewhat marginalize vampires is super cool, but how big a threat are vampires in the era of the Savage Age? Being a vampire in this era would be really difficult considering how few people there are and how dangerous travel would be for them. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I still don\u2019t really like the idea that the Mokole have genocided an unknown number of different Fera species over the ages. It feels like something that would have earned them the ire of powerful spirits, including possibly Gaia herself. Even if the \u201cmodern\u201d Mokole have moved beyond the idea, their ancient sins should be a problem for them.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The details of how the Khara dealt with secrets is useful and the potential for a secret the Khara thought safely buried away could be useful in a campaign, even one set in modern times.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">There is a weird formatting thing where the On killing secrets section starts on a page with other information, but the On language and painting section starts on a new page leaving another half empty page.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The on language section is very interesting and the Khara\u2019s inability to control a secret from spreading being their downfall is a very cool idea. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The idea that some percentage of Caerns feed on the energy of powerful secrets is cool, and could be fodder for a plotline or two. A powerful enemy attacking a Caern to get to the secret buried deep beneath it, or someone other than the Garou building on the site to harness the energy\u2026 </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The last bit on the death of the Khara species is interesting, particularly the Balam-Naghaluu wars.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I am slightly confused about why chapters 1 and 2 aren\u2019t a single chapter. The stuff covered by chapter 2 isn\u2019t purely about secret management.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Chapter 3: Who They Were</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I found the information in this section good, but wanted more about how they interacted with their kinfolk. The idea that they preferred animal kin and breeds over Neanderthal and Homo Sapiens makes sense. The relations with other Baset and Fera discussed here make sense. Also I feel like there should be mention somewhere of the circumstances that could lead to even a temporary pack of Khara assembling; surely some secrets were too big, too dangerous for a single Khara to suppress. </span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Chapter 4: Breath of Life</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Now we get into the meat of the book, all the basic information including the Yava is good. The expansion of the secrets and Den-realm backgrounds both make sense for the Khara. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">There are 18 gifts listed for the Khara, a mix of 7 re-used ones and the other 11 new. Interestingly the two starting gifts listed are not part of this 18 so it is really 20 gifts. Not super keen on having to track down where the re-used gifts are but it is clear from Accounting for the Dead that the writers realized that and added references in later books. One thing I would like clarified is if Khara can take general Baset gifts?</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Lots of interesting Gifts here, though the write up for Seline\u2019s Mercy is odd, the gift it mirrors directly contradicts the flavour text. There is a good mix of combat and non-combat gifts. I worry that Piercing Bite is overpowered for a rank 3 gift, completely ignoring soak gives a combat oriented Khara a decent chance to 1 shot many foes. I am also not keen on Khara getting a Corax rank 5 at rank 4.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Combined with the 23 gifts in Accounting for the Dead, you have lots of options. My concerns about some of those gifts remains but it is a healthy selection.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">This chapter also has 5 rites, one of each level. All are interesting, but the two most powerful rites don\u2019t seem like things the players would frequently or ever use.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The 5 new fetishes listed are all interesting and useful in different ways. Several of the non-combat ones could get your players very paranoid. The Level 4 one especially could lead to some interesting story hooks. I assume it is their spirit that is bound within and breaking it would not result in a Khara suddenly showing up very angry at you if it broke.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The two totems are cool and providing a way to use some of the gifts in a modern campaign is a good idea. A rival Garou pack devoted to Homotherium could be an interesting problem for players to have to deal with, especially if they uncovered something that should have been left forgotten in an earlier age\u2026</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Ending this chapter are 4 general character concepts, a good idea that helps establish some of the types of Khara characters that could be out there.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Appendix</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Having some info about sabre-toothed cats/tigers is good, and helps paint the picture of the type of environment the Khara operated in.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The ideas for modern Khara offer some cool ideas. Some of which could work just as well for NPCs or storylines for the players to handle. The small pool of Khara kinfolk that very rarely get a true Khara show up could be a weird isolated community that the players stumble into, maybe the only Khara around is still very young, only months or a year after their first change. </span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Overall Thoughts</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I think for the size and cost of the book, you get a decent amount assuming you have other Savage Age books. I think you really need the extra options provided by Accounting for the Dead to really make good use of the Khara. I think it would have been better for both products if the Khara section of Accounting for the Dead was part of this book, with an appropriate price adjustment, and that space used to expand the other parts of Accounting for the Dead. Without Accounting for the Dead I think you could only have 1 Khara in the party or use them as NPCs. If the Savage Age hadn\u2019t continued I don\u2019t know how useful this book would be beyond an NPC for a short story arc or to augment a Shattered Dreams game. Thankfully there is an entire line of Savage Age products so that isn\u2019t an issue.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">One thing I wish that could have been explored but wasn\u2019t is: What happens when too much forbidden knowledge and secrets break a Khara? We rarely get more than general hints at fallen members of various Fera and this would have been an interesting bit to add.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Like with Accounting for the Dead the art is excellent.</span></span><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12161-browman","name":"Browman","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"135"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-05-28T17:02:13-07:00","id":1551331,"title":"","content":"<b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Tribebook: Naghaluu</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Even bigger and badder wolves for Fera to be connected to? What could go wrong with that idea? Also it is good to see that this book is 70 pages vs the 44 in Tribebook: Khara. This difference matters even more when you consider that every book loses a few pages to covers, tables of contents and such; in the case of Savage Age books that seems to be 5 pages. Nothing wrong with that especially since it includes two good, full page art pieces. But for actual content it means 65 pages vs 39 pages or a 60% increase. This probably also explains why there is no need for a Naghaluu section in Accounting for the dead.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Prologue: Caliah</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Like with Tribebook: Khara the four short stories at the start of the book do a good job of establishing the general idea of the Naghaluu. And of course modern Garou just assume they were Garou not a different Fera.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Chapter 1: Tribal Background</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The ideas about the Wyld being far more dangerous in the Savage Age are cool and make sense, but if they are so dangerous, who is policing them in the Old World if the Naghaluu are only in the New World? The idea that they could be Wyld corrupted is also interesting. The reasoning for passing on the name of the Wyld corrupted Apuray to the Camazotz is cool, but should have been explained more. If their own pack is hunting them then why do the Were-bats need to be involved?</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The visions and prophecy interest is interesting as I would think truly powerful Wyld threats would largely confound such attempts to predict the future.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Their use of powers from all three of the Triat is an interesting idea. I think it helps reinforce the idea that every time a Fera is wiped out, it makes it harder and harder for the surviving ones to perform their Gaia appointed tasks. I remain frustrated when chapters end with nearly empty pages.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Naghaluu hating Ratkin makes sense, since the were-rats are wyld-mad or at least they are in modern times. Various sources have suggested that got worse by their exile after the War of Rage. I don\u2019t think they are as far gone in this era.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">This section also does a good job of tying into stuff mentioned in Tribebook: Khara and that the Garou wiping out the old world Khara has ripples across the world. And I like how it is isn\u2019t just using Savage Age specific Fera, with various Baset, Ratkin and Mokole involved. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Detailing the War of Many Teeth is good, and could easily serve as the backdrop for a pure lands campaign, mostly simmering the background but causes shifts in the opinion and relations with various Fera NPCs before bursting into full force at the climax of the campaign, possibly even ending the campaign with the player characters getting to fight attack after attack by once allied Fera. The Ratkin starting the war makes complete sense. The war becoming uglier and uglier as time goes on also makes sense. There is enough detail here to use the war, but not so much that it would limit a storyteller in what they wanted to do. The final tidbit about the last known survivors fleeing to antarctica pursued by some ratkin also offers some story hooks, potentially even for campaigns set in modern times.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The brief bit about the Tengger, the forerunner to the 3 brothers of the Garou tribes is cool and I think the most information we have gotten so far in the Savage age about any proto-Garou groups other than the Anupu.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Chapter 2: On the Nature of Betrayal</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The first part of this doesn\u2019t seem to actually give much information for the amount of words used. Though the ideas of separate until a pack was needed and clarity of purpose to cut through the chaos of the Wyld are cool ideas. Their interactions with other Fera makes the inevitable War of Many Teeth more tragic. The idea of rites being important to their combat style is cool, I am interested to see how that works out later in the book</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The bits about what various Fera remember about the Naghaluu is cool, but I don\u2019t know how useful pages of what various Garou tribes know in modern times is. The Lodge of Nine Knives is a cool concept, more small groups of different Fera should exist that aren\u2019t as big as the Asian or African groupings.</span></span><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12161-browman","name":"Browman","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"135"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-05-29T17:20:07-07:00","id":1551338,"title":"","content":"<b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Chapter 3: Who They Were</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Organizationally I would have preferred this earlier in the book, before On the Nature of Betrayal. This is the Naghaluu book after all, I should know about them before I learn about how other Fera deal with their loss.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">That being said, this chapter has lots of useful info, distribution across the pure lands, being more lupus than homid. And I feel like some of the detail here is what was missing from the Khara book. We have details on how their packs operated, though I think in this case when the book says pack, it is more like sept in the Garou understanding of organization. I assume the numbers given here are Naghaluu and kinfolk combined. It does seem odd to spend months picking an alpha who is only alpha for a year.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Having information about how neighbouring packs interact is also useful, since this would likely come up in any Naghaluu campaign.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The page about how Naghaluu interact with other Fera is useful and reinforces my view that too much time in chapter 2 was spent on what more modern Fera remember/ know about them.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The relations with Luna section is very interesting and should help differentiate the Naghaluu from the Garou. Moonbridges without needing a fixed end point are really cool and offer interesting options, including ending up somewhere you didn\u2019t want to, especially if you are trying to get too close to some Wyld incursion that is messing with things. I am curious how that will work mechanically.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">A litany for the Naghaluu is a great idea and really helps any players looking to play a were-dire wolf. And I like how it isn\u2019t just a simple port of the Garou litany.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Chapter 4: Breath of Life</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The core mechanical stuff all seems good. I appreciate that some time was taken to explain how Apuray works. Similarly detailing how they rage, frenzy and rapture is also useful. I also like that they don\u2019t use the same renown set as Garou.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The gift list is extensive with 3 breed lists, a general list and 2 \u201cAuspice\u201d lists. 37 Breed gifts, a little more than half re-used. 9 on the general list only 3 re-used. 35 between the two \u201cAuspice\u201d lists, 26 of them re-used. Over 80 gifts gives lots of options making running a pack of all Naghaluu viable. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Seekers Eye is a cool gift. Isn\u2019t Gaian Tongue just a slightly re-worked Speech of the World? Why isn\u2019t it indicated as such? Howl of the pack is cool, and could be used by the storyteller to initiate something. Umbral Sense\u2019s range is odd, even at high ranks you are talking less than 2 meters which is not that helpful. I assume something is missing here and it is supposed to be like Rank x 10 feet or something. It is mildly annoying that the re-used gifts in this section sometimes just say Garou gift and other times call out what auspice, tribe or breed has the gift. Channel chaos is a really cool gift. Puissance of the Raging River is a cool but powerful gift.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Overall the gifts section is good. I think the addition of two auspices is a good idea, though I much prefer the gifts given by Thunder Moon over Full Cold Moon for my first character idea.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">There are 7 rites, interestingly none at level 2. Beseeching Mother Luna is a cool idea but effectively is a resource tax, isn\u2019t everyone going to have to take this so they can pick an auspice? Given what was said earlier I was expecting more than one combat prep rite, the one rite is cool though. I wish there was more detail about Calming the Madness. The rite to actually create a caern is really detailed and elaborate, using that rite is like an entire gaming session. I feel like this one should be similar. Closing a Wyld Abscess should be an epic event, this doesn\u2019t feel it would be that. Great Lunar Bridge is a cool rite, powerful but with the cost not something you can use every day.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">A small number of merits and one flaw are also provided, all are useful for one character concept or another and none seem overpowered. The page with the flaw is a good example of how to have only a few words on a page, the rune technically only takes a small amount of space but makes the page feel usefully used.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">There are two fetishes, and the Hunting Spear one is slightly confusing to me. Is it just a regular spear dealing lethal damage with a slightly better hit chance? That doesn\u2019t seem worth 2 levels of fetish.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The two totems are interesting but don\u2019t seem to be anything special.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The two character concepts are good but I would have liked more of them and in Tribebook: Khara there were suggestions for attribute and ability spreads which I would have preferred.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Appendix</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Like with Tribebook: Khara, having a section on actual real life dire wolves is useful. The ideas for how to potentially use them in modern nights is interesting.</span></span><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12161-browman","name":"Browman","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"135"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-05-30T17:35:52-07:00","id":1551354,"title":"","content":"<b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Tribebook: Ceilican</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Ceilican got done kind of dirty by WtA, the idea behind them was kind of odd in the first place, then mostly got killed off and/or corrupted in revised then that got undone in W20 Changing Breeds. It has often felt like the writers of WtA never really knew what to do with this group of Bastet. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Perhaps this is only 50 pages because there is already a lot more content for the Ceilican out there compared with the Khara and Naghaluu. It is priced closer to the Khara than Naghaluu. I will be honest out of the 4 books in the Savage Age Intro bundle, this is one I was least interested in, the Ceilican were probably the last Bastet I would be interested in playing.</span></span> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Prologue: Caliah</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I like the two slightly longer stories presented in this book. Both do a good job of showing the character of the average Ceilican. </span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Chapter 1: Tribal Background</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Ceilican being more of a philosophy rather than a Bastet tribe tied to a specific great cat breed. There is lots of information in this section that would be useful for playing a Savage Age Ceilican. The hints of what happened in the War of Rage in Europe are interesting and show that the people behind the Savage Age were realizing that they needed to start detailing that rather than talking around it like they have in the other, slightly earlier books. I hope this Ritual of Dark Taking and related stuff is significantly elaborated on in the Garou book Savage Age did later.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Of course negotiating with the Garou didn\u2019t work, far too many Garou rage anytime they have to deal with something they don\u2019t understand or don\u2019t immediately get their way. They are like young children who get told no.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Knowing that dealing with the Fae was a trap but having really no other option is I think a good way to handle them. </span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Chapter 2: On the Nature of Secrets</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Their interest in secrets of place, not as much people or animals is interesting. It also explains how they ended up exploring all sorts of odd corners of reality and coming to the attention of the Fae. Them becoming interested in humans only once real civilization started developing is logical and interesting. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The stuff about their interactions with the Fae is interesting but not really detailed. Formatting wise, there is a weird amount of white space on the first page talking about their interaction with the Fae.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Chapter 3: Who They Were</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">This chapter seems to be playing a bit fast and loose with the timeline as the cat is \u201cdomesticated\u201d thousands of years after dogs and probably after the War of Rage. Like most Bastet, they are generally solitary, which makes fighting the Garou or proto-Garou difficult. Especially when the Ceilican aren\u2019t exactly the most combat oriented of the Fera.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Like with any Bastet detailing the types of secrets they most seek is useful.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The relations detailed with other Fera make sense.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Chapter 4: Breath of Life</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Now to the meat of the book. I don\u2019t feel like their Yava were really covered in the earlier sections, especially the last one. That is literally never mentioned earlier in this book and it would impact them significantly. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The option of picking between two animal forms is cool and logical for the Ceilican. Again there is a half empty page.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The backgrounds expansion is the same as that in Tribebook: Khara and again makes sense here.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I like that they are calling out which gifts are tied to the Fae or not. That makes it easier to run a pre-Fae character, but also makes this book more useful outside the Savage Age. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">In total there are 25 gifts listed here, with no overlap with the W20 Changing Breeds list. 10 of those gifts are reused from other sources. Hunter\u2019s Mask is a super cool gift. Mother\u2019s blessing/curse seems more like an NPC gift. Sorcerer\u2019s Blade is a very useful ability especially for those like the Ceilican who don\u2019t have many combat gifts and as far as I can tell the Ceilican doesn\u2019t have be the one actually wielding the blade for it to work so room for some scheming and cunning. Recall the Great Form is also a cool gift and by giving the stats for the original Ceilican forms also allows for those to be used by default in truly ancient games.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Adding in gifts that let them take some actual Fae abilities makes complete sense, though it does require you to have access to the Fae book. Which if playing a Ceilican is a big deal for you, you almost certainly would have. I do feel like Advanced Glamour should be a level 5 gift though.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Overall I really like this list of gifts, it really plays into the way the Ceilican are presented both here and in other sources. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">There are 5 rites presented here all the way up to a level 6 rite which is super cool. Organizationally I would have put Generational Smile after the Connection rites, so that you have the level 1 rites first. I do like that most of the rites are lower level with the one really powerful advanced rite that shows how they eventually broke free of the Fae. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The one talens and one fetish are both cool and useful for the Ceilican (and likely other Fera).</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Four totems are given instead of the usual two that previous books have had. They are very interesting and offer some cool benefits, but also interesting drawbacks. Of course one of the them had to be a Fae. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The four character concepts are cool, but like with the Naghaluu I wish they included suggestions for attribute and ability distribution. Those can really help fairly new players.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Appendix</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">As always the little bit of real world information about the species of animals the Ceilican are tied to is useful. Personally I don\u2019t like this books interpretation of the Ceilican in the modern era and I would ignore that stuff in a game I was running that used them.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The index doesn\u2019t seem that useful having only 12 entries.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Overall Thoughts</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Of the four books in the Savage Age Intro Bundle, this book was the one that least interested me and it remains that way after reading all of them. People who like the Ceilican and want to use them in a campaign will find this book super useful, either for a Savage Age game or even a more modern game. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">It feels like I am repeating myself, but the art is still really good.</span></span><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12161-browman","name":"Browman","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"135"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-05-31T16:11:07-07:00","id":1551369,"title":"","content":"<b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Thoughts about the Savage Age Intro Bundle as a whole</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I firmly believe this bundle is better than any of the parts individually. Accounting for the dead gives a bunch of background stuff and combined with Tribebook: Khara gives you 4 solid Savage Age species you can easily use in a game set in this era. The other two tribe books really help round out the understanding of the era to make running games easier, and with Naghaluu probably gives you everything you need to run a pure lands campaign. Tribebook Ceilican is probably the least needed of all of them, but still gives some information about what the proto-Garou are up to in Europe and some other tidbits. Even if you don\u2019t have either Khara, Naghaluu or Ceilican player characters their tribebooks give plenty of ways they could show up as NPCs or be impacting the wider story around the characters. A Naghaluu moon bridge raid to claim human kinfolk could easily be the initiating event of a campaign or throw the status quo up in the air. Player characters are often doing crazy things, so a Khara could have decided that perhaps they learned something they shouldn\u2019t have. A Ceilican showing up might be a good way for the storyteller to give players some information they should have learned before but didn\u2019t.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">A lot of work went into making dozens of gifts and even just figuring out what existing gifts are appropriate for new Fera.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I think it speaks to how much I enjoyed reading through these books and my intent to run a Savage Age game that the same day I finished reading Tribebook: Ceilican I bought the War of Rage bundle (Storyteller vault is set up so that if you buy a bundle with stuff you already own it doesn\u2019t include them in the price), which gave me two additional books, The Savage Age Volume 2: Rise of the Garou and Tribebook: Wlewa. I felt like I need the Garou side of this time to really fill out the setting. Look for my detailed thoughts on those two books shortly.</span></span><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12161-browman","name":"Browman","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"135"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-06-03T16:58:53-07:00","id":1551450,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Now I am onto the two other books from the War of Rage Bundle, starting with the larger book. </span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Savage Age Volume 2: Rise of the Garou</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">This book is 35% bigger than the Savage Age Volume 1, which was already the biggest and best book of the intro bundle. I am very interested to see how they have built the proto-Garou and their version of the War of Rage. There have been lots of hints and small pieces but now it should all come together. I understand why it took releasing a few other books first to get to this point as the state of the proto-Garou is not something you want to rush.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">In Volume 1 we had a prologue and 6 chapters, now we have 12 entries in the table of contents and no prologue. Also since Volume 1 covered some key generic Savage Age gaming, we don\u2019t need page count for that.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">A Brief History of the Formation of the Garou Nation</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The title of this chapter feels like the title of the chapter of a history textbook, which I think is a good vibe to be on for the lore portions of a book like this.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">We are starting the War of Rage in India, very interesting. It has very much been suggested by earlier Savage Age products that the Garou are much more splintered in this era and now we get a clearer picture of that. I think it makes things more interesting that at this point the Garou are closer to how the Bastet work in some ways than the modern Garou. The ideas suggested here make me really want some Savage Age: Rage Across ____ books that give some real details about various places at key points in Savage Age history. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Like many real world conflicts, webs of intergenerational feuds, unwillingness to see the foe as an equal and complex alliance structures slowly see more and more groups drawn into the conflict as it continues. Until you reach a point where only one side can truly survive, whatever remains of the other will be a shattered remnant that barely resembles what it was before.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The page or so about the Garou nation forming is very interesting and logical.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I am not fully sold on the idea of Saksom Akami joining the Garou and immediately allowing themselves to be used as sacrificial shock troops. Hopefully that will be explained in more detail later in the book. I feel like all the non-canid in the Garou Nation dying off was not entirely accidental/coincidental. From a gameplay point of view, I can see the value in having some non-Garou options to add to packs from this side of the War.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The loss of other canid forcing all the proto-Garou towards the wolf also makes sense. Though I would like a better explanation of how so many Fera were much more able to shift their animal kin in this era.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I should note before moving on that I am pretty sure both the Saksom Akami and Wlewa are newly created for this book.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Lineages</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Now that the stage is set, we start getting into the details of the various groups in the Garou side of this war. The idea that different proto/pre-Garou canid-shifters had different Gaian tasks is interesting, and possibly another thing that the War of Rage broke that is still causing problems in modern times. The chart of canid-shifter to modern Garou tribes is very useful, but I think the Tengger is missing the Uktena as a primary descendant.</span></span> <b><span style=\"color:#434343\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Bilai</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Ah so we are getting a kind of prologue story for each lineage it would seem. And we see the beginning of the Get; a cool story. The Bilai are interesting, like modern Garou in some ways, but seemingly less driven by their rage. Being Neanderthal focused at first is interesting, and leads to the potential for groups of Neadherthal Kinfolk to survive past the end of most of their branch of humanity.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Their relations with other Fera make sense. Though European Ajaba? Tell me more.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">It feels in some ways like the Bilai are primarily two auspices, Galliard and Ahroun, I wonder if that feel of each proto-Garou will feel similar to that, seeming to focus on a couple auspices.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Regional sub-Bilai having different names helps reinforce that these guys will split into multiple tribes down the line.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The tidbit about ~10% of the Bilai falling to the Wyrm is also interesting, and is hopefully expanded on. Also foreshadowing the White Howlers future. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The section about them in the War of Rage is very good, with them being very much out for glory and anyone not on our side is an enemy in waiting.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The idea of a modern Bilai seems odd, wouldn\u2019t they just get claimed by one modern tribe or another? They were still actual werewolves unlike most parts of the proto-Garou. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">No Bilai Metis, right, and they never fall to the Wyrm or unjustly lash out at other Fera. I assume the Bilai kill any Metis that they discover. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Bilai have 24 gifts specifically mentioned, along with a note that other gifts from their descendant tribes should be considered as well. Of the 24 listed, 13 are re-used mostly from Fianna and Get of Fenris lists. I like the mix of gifts, thought I am not keen on getting Halt the Coward\u2019s Flight and Hero\u2019s stand a rank earlier.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Bilai seem interesting and seem like a good option for someone who wants to play something close to a modern Garou.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#434343\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Siberakh</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Another excellent story at the start of the Siberakh section. A spiral of madness you say. Pure white fur as a sign of Gaia\u2019s blessing. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Even other proto-Garou consider them to be fanatics, exactly what is needed to bring down tensions. I am sure as proto-Garou they will never jump to conclusions about people being Wyrm tainted. Their initial section gives off a lot of \u201csome you may die, but it is a sacrifice I am willing to make\u201d vibes.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">What is this ghost lineage that is mentioned with various humans? I think this is the first time we have heard any hint of this. Other than that the info about all their kinfolk make sense. If I understand things correctly their animal kin is a very close relative of modern wolves.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Their relations with other Fera make sense, they and the Grondr should work together but Fera, especially Garou, even proto-Garou struggle to play nice together. Of course the proto-Silver Fangs want the Apis to give them perfect bloodlines.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Their participation in the war of rage is well detailed. At this point their main auspices seem to be Theurge, Philodox and Ahroun. Starting the Impergium out of a wish to reduce Wyrm taint is logical. Also it seems like these guys ultimately fared better than the Bilai did until the land bridge with the Americas was broken.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I didn\u2019t get a strong Ragabash vibe from their write-up. Again \u201cno\u201d Metis, I wonder if all the proto-Garou will say this. I can see the argument pre-War of Rage but I would think during it they would at least use the Metis as spear fodder.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Siberakh have 26 gifts in their list, including 16 re-used ones. Invoke the Delirium is a cool ability for this era and makes sense for the group that started the Impergium. Not super keen on them getting a rank 5 Wendigo gift at rank 4. Death\u2019s Chilled Hide, Misery\u2019s End and the Lord is the Land are all cool gifts. </span></span><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12161-browman","name":"Browman","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"135"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-06-04T19:14:44-07:00","id":1551468,"title":"","content":"<b><span style=\"color:#434343\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Atzu Telah Nahau</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">As usual the short story at the start is a good introduction to the group being discussed next. These guys immediately seem very Theurge focused. One thing I am not super keen on that the Savage Age does is have groups like this with long names that end up being shortened anyway, if they are generally going to be called the Nahau, why not just call them that.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">These guys were mentioned in the Ceilican book as cooking up some super ritual, hopefully we will get more details on that.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">As a more intellectual canid-shifter, it makes sense they were hesitant to get drawn into the War of Rage. I think their second auspice focus is going to be either Ragabash or Philodox.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The kinfolk section is interesting, I would have thought they would have mostly chosen homid kinfolk that were shamans or priests.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Their relations with other Fera make sense especially the becoming obsessive with the worry that the Bastet are going to steal their secrets. A legitimate worry. My guess is the Khara especially put down some of these guys who learned the wrong things.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Their internal variations make sense, though we get another mention of the Ritual of Dark Taking with no real details.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Now that we are in the War of Rage section it would seem we are going to get details on this super ritual. Taking five generations to figure out and complete is certainly an undertaking, one I doubt the modern Garou could actually do. The impact on the War of Rage is well laid out, but it isn\u2019t actually clear to me what the primary effect of the mist is; did it actually work to lock them into the form they were in when they encountered the mist? Still an entire campaign could be built around just a part of such a great ritual.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Again \u201cno\u201d Metis. Creating Metis is still forbidden in the modern times and it still happens. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">32 gifts in total, 20 of them re-used. There are lots of cool magical abilities in this list of gifts both new and re-used, some are clearly not combat or in some cases obviously useful for player characters but still good for showing the range of what the Nahau are capable of. I do wonder though if Fertile Lands should have been a rite. With some thought Visions from the Pool has lots of cool uses. Communal Spirit is a gift that would be very helpful to the modern Garou. Healing Breath of Mother Gaia seems like it is leaning heavily into Grondr or Gurahl territory, not sure if that is a good thing without some explanation like the Nahau stealing it.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#434343\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Kucha-Ekundu/Sakurai</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I find it interesting that the Savage Age is flipping it so that these guys are the original similar to the Siberakh, especially considering the groups that retained those names aren\u2019t part of the Garou Nation. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">It fits for the forebears of the Red Talons to be all about the pack.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I appreciate that in an era where there were more canids around, not all the \u201cWerewolves\u201d primarily used Canis Lupus as their animal kin. That also suggests that other similar modern Fera could get absorbed by the Garou in the future. It is also interesting that those who will become the Red Talons are some of the last to shift to the modern wolf.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">A proto-Garou pushing themselves too far so that they are becoming regionally extinct seems entirely on brand. And of course other Fera not following their lead was unacceptable.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The info about their kinfolk is useful and logical. As is the info about their relations with other Fera. The Kucha and various Bastet tribes being terrible I think is mostly a result of sharing the same territory and presumably homid kinfolk and their animal kinfolk often competing.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The tribal variations make sense, I think it shows how bad being on the front lines of the war of rage was that the two main groups are either still only a handful of packs or had to flee to Australia (and then get wiped out by the Garou).</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">It makes complete sense for the Kucha to be against including any non-canids in the Garou alliance. The bit on them in the modern nights is interesting, but I would have liked more detail on the current state of the Kucha Ekundu. Most sources have little more than \u201cthese guys exist\u201d; I think their numbers are incredibly low, like single digit number of packs low, but that is more a guess based on what has been written about them than anything concrete.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">There are 24 Kucha gifts, half new, half-reused, mostly red talon. Each of their two sub-groups then have an additional 3 rank 1 gifts each. All the gifts seem on theme, but several are granted below their normal rank which I rarely think is a good idea. Speak to the Land and Mark as Prey seem particularly interesting.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#434343\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Saskom Aknami</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Gurahl are one of my favorite Fera, so getting a new tribe of them is great. The idea of a tribe of Gurahl being part of the Garou Nation is also super cool and would have wildly changed that group if they survived to the modern era. And their Gaian task sounds cool.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The story as usual is solid. As usual most Fera don\u2019t understand and underestimate what other Fera do.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I like how they take a very direct role leading kinfolk and human communities. It makes them somewhat different from a lot of other Fera and other Gurahl tribes. Being tied to the Wyrm of Balance is also probably not going to go well as the Wyrm becomes more and more insane and broken. Strength through sacrifice sounds like a good idea but can often be taken too far. Their quasi-religion is interesting and the mimicking by their Neanderthal kin is a dark but interesting follow on, that probably won\u2019t end well. Being much more focused on Neanderthals over Sapiens will probably not work out well long term.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Their relations with other Fera make sense, the sometimes cooperation with the Grondr was something I didn\u2019t expect. I also would have liked a little more detail in their pre-Garou Nation relations with the various canid Fera.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The war of rage part is interesting especially the stuff about slowly seeming to shift more towards corrupted aspects of the Wyrm, but I think misses the important bit, why exactly the Saskom Aknami die out in the war.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The idea of them in the modern nights is interesting. I think you could also have a small group of them existing as Wyrm corrupted bears that survived in very limited numbers to modern nights, augmented by a limited number of other Gurahl that have fallen over the millenia.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Only a handful of gifts for the Saskom Aknami. They are all cool, teleporting Werebear is terrifying Werebear.</span></span><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12161-browman","name":"Browman","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"135"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-06-05T17:46:36-07:00","id":1551487,"title":"","content":"<b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Age of Lupus</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">It makes sense that the homid and lupus sides of most Fera are much more balanced in this era, the modern era is possibly as bad as it is because of how skewed the ratios are for most Fera by that point. That being said, I would like it if they mentioned which areas are more lupus or homid dominated in this era. Sometimes it feels like the Savage Age is afraid to nail anything down with too much detail; but that pushes work onto the storyteller.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The bits about how lupus characters may be different are useful and a good reminder that using multiple senses to describe things is often very useful. In some ways more Fera being lupus or equivalent helps explain how the War of Rage happened, since they seem less worried about the future, or thinking through the second and third order effects of doing something, they are less likely to see the impact that losing entire Fera species will have.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Tools, Structures and Innovation in the Savage Age</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I found the general information provided in previous books helpful for setting the stage for the Savage age and this will cover a key aspect of life in that era. With humans much more successful in specific parts of the world, primarily the fertile crescent, and the major river valleys of India and China, Fera will be congregated in and around those areas as well. With probably not enough space to go around, conflict becomes almost inevitable.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Cities in the low 10s of thousands seems like more than what happened in real life, but the Savage Age is likely taking some liberties with our understanding of this era for sake of some gameplay options. Those cities will among other things be drawing the ratkin and first generations of vampires.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I wonder how the various groups of canid-shifters feel about the domestication of the dog. I can see significant opposition from some corners, but others that see it as way to get canid and human kinfolk in one grouping, perhaps not realizing what will happen to their canid kinfolk over generations. Are the Bastet and other non-canid Fera trying to use this to weaken the various proto-Garou?</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">If you know what you are doing you can make lots of cool tools out of stone, and cold forging stuff is a huge pain by my understanding. The wheel is one of the greatest and most significant inventions. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Spears have been a primary weapon up until humans made very good guns (with bayonets so they are basically gun spears). I would think other common weapons in this era would be stone axes or mauls, except for supernatural stuff blunt force trauma is really hard to treat or defend from. Both probably also have other uses, like axes cutting down trees.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The other details in this section are useful.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Totems in the Ice Age</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">This is a huge list of Savage Age totems, 28 actually stated out and another 5 and half pages of where to find other ones. That is about as extensive a list as you could possibly get. I like the range of these. Some are cheap, as few as 3 totem cost, others cost as much as twelve. Some grant really powerful abilities, but also have huge drawbacks. Some would give very solid in game reasons why powerful NPCs the players meet can\u2019t help them all the time do to the bans of their totems. Some seem very Wyrm adjacent. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">All four totems of cunning are great, with interesting traits and bans, my favorite is probably Danus the River. Its traits are useful and interesting without being super over powered and its ban is limiting but not impossible to follow.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The glory totems are interesting, and most would be a great totem to give a rival pack that is constantly trying to steal the limelight with the larger community from the PCs.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">With the totems of respect I like how Spring mirrors the Autumn totem in many ways. All the bans could easily be used to set a story in motion. Baobab is a good, cheap totem.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The totems of war are generally powerful but come with bans that could make things difficult for those who chose them as totems.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Jemos is probably the most interesting of the totems of wisdom but all of them could be useful to the right party. Winter requiring never asking or answering a direct question sounds like something a bunch of Fera would love.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Birch is a great cheap totem, possibly too cheap since you get two common gifts from it and its ban is not difficult.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Gifts and Gaian Mandate</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">This is a useful 1 page section that helps reinforce that the Savage Age is not the modern era. Forcing players to actually pay attention to the mandate of their kind is useful, especially since it may lead to mixed packs having to work out how to follow all their mandates simultaneously which may be challenging.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Character Sheet and Character Creation</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">This is another short but useful section, there are lots of ability changes in the Savage Age compared to modern so a rundown of them is very useful. </span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Overall thoughts</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">This is clearly the most ambitious Savage Age book to date and one that easily could have derailed the whole line if it wasn\u2019t done well. Fortunately this is an excellent book. All the various proto-Garou are interesting and the possibility of a Gurahl tribe in the Garou Nation is super cool. This book I think is the most stand alone of all the Savage Age books so far, though is certainly made better with Accounting for the Dead and the various tribebooks. My modern era campaign may need to see a Saskom Aknami founded Wyrm corrupted Gurahl group that is scary enough that even the Black Spiral Dancers don\u2019t mess with them.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Like with the Savage Age Volume 1, the biggest editing issue seems to be not double checking their references for where to find other gifts.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I would have liked to see some rites, fetishes and talens, especially since some of the canid are very mystically inclined. I get that they released a full book on this type of stuff later the same year, but it still feels odd that there are none.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I wanted more detail on the death of the Saskom Aknami. Did some of the canid-Fera deliberately send them into situations to get them killed? Did the proto-Garou themselves kill off the last of them for refusing to give them the healing and resurrection gifts and rites they so wanted? Was it simply a matter of their animal and neanderthal kin dying out that doomed them? Was there a specific final one who knew he was the last of his kind? Did the Fera fighting against the Garou in the War of Rage target them deliberately as a message that no one else could join with canids?</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Ultimately if you want to game in the Savage Age, you want this book.</span></span><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12161-browman","name":"Browman","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"135"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-06-06T17:33:44-07:00","id":1551508,"title":"","content":"<b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Tribebook: Wlewa</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The judges of secrets, is a subtitle that goes hard. Their Gaian mandate couldn\u2019t possibly be used for ill.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The two versions of one story is cool for a group of secret keepers and judgers.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Tribal Background</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The art on the first page of this section is particularly great. Being the first Fera to get a tribal totem is cool. And we have seen with Black Tooth and friends, how dangerous organized groups of Bastet can be. The pre-War of Rage them acting as actual judges in intra-Fera disputes is cool, and could be the starting point of a really cool story arc in a campaign. That would be a great way to introduce a bunch of NPC Fera of various species as they all come to bear witness to such a trial.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The other Fera eventually shifting because of the Wlewa\u2019s actions to \u201cOh war with the Canids. Good luck with that.\u201d is good. Being in both Europe and North America is interesting since the proto-Garou aren\u2019t really in the Americas yet. It will be interesting to see what happens to the American branch of the tribe. Also not paying attention to their animal kin side is interesting for the Savage Age, others were able to shift species and there were still plenty of related great cats in this era; the regular lion range was much larger back then if nothing else.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The write-up of how they operate is very similar to how Black Tooth\u2019s pride was said to work with a mixture of both human and animal kin attached, which seems odd. Humans would not be comfortable around multiple cave lions unless the Wlewa can prevent the cave lions from attacking them.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">All of the Fera are proud, none of them will take kindly to being judged, unless the outcome is obvious that they really, really messed up. Even then most Fera will remember that as a slight to their dying day. At the same time, Fera generally respect strength. The only way the Wlewa can maintain their system is if they remain a top dog.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">And the most common fault of the Fera, arrogance, which is the root of many of the problems they have. Not shocking that it came for the Wlewa as well.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Eldritch magic you say, tell me more. Preferably with mechanics that can easily be used to give to other Fera magic users.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Beringia seems like an unfortunate place to have some of your key organization in. Again it sounds like these guys are strong in North America so what went wrong for them there.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">It feels odd in the culture section to say that they favoured their animal kin when earlier it said they weren\u2019t paying attention while their animal kin died out. The Hamaal feels like a form of feudalism from the initial descriptions.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Their views on all the other Fera make sense for the Wlewa. The expanded detail on their conflicts with the various Bastet and proto-Garou is good and makes sense. Using the Apis\u2019s openness as cover for magical study is a cool detail.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Who are these new Bastet mentioned in the Hamaal section? Also the Hamaal is sounding more like serfdom/slavery disguised as something more noble.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Judges of Secrets</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">We get some good detail here about the types of stuff that the Wlewa were looking for, and suggests that there are Wlewa doing different things. You have investigating Wlewa, judging Wlewa and at least later, magic lore Wlewa. Judges probably also need guards and knowledge keepers. Somehow I don\u2019t think the Wlewa noticed or cared at the humiliation that other Fera would feel being forced to face judgement over something.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The way the Wlewa operate seems very much in the power corrupts category. It sounds like they were doing technically correct (at least by their understanding) while completely ignoring most other aspects of being a good judge.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Bastet being willing to work with the Wlewa on preserving Yava is a cool detail.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Mantle of Leadership</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Hereditary leadership is interesting for them. I guess it kind of fits with the, they were made to rule over other Fera, so some of them need to rule over the rest.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The sebios system basically sounds like feudalism, combined with the Hamaal as basically a serf class at the bottom. I like that we actually get some details of the 3 Grand Sebios that exist by the War of Rage. One of my biggest issues with earlier Savage Age books has been an unwillingness to get into specifics. The Hamaal starting as a less corrupt thing than it became also makes sense. If they had gone straight for the final version there would have been a lot more resistance from the start.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">It looks like we are getting details of some other variations of Bastet, the Saa appear to be North American Simba, others Bastet aligned with various big cats that go extinct over the next few thousand years. And we get a mention of Bubasti necromancers, which is cool and I don\u2019t think it's a type of magic they generally use in the modern era. Lots of cool detail about how the different Basetet feel about the Hamaal. There is some really cool art at the end of this section.</span></span><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12161-browman","name":"Browman","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"135"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-06-09T17:58:35-07:00","id":1551552,"title":"","content":"<b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Wlewa\u2019s War of Rage</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Wlewa\u2019s self-righteous attitude means they are at odds with lots of Fera, and are in basically open warfare with most of the proto-Garou and Simba even before the War of Rage really becomes a thing. The idea of Cave Bridges is super cool. It seems in many ways like the Wlewa were so sure of their superiority that they didn\u2019t actually press their initial advantage, and having to fight a war on many fronts meant that they couldn\u2019t concentrate their forces.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Rites being important to their early victories is also cool. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Now we get a more detailed description of the fall of the Saksom Aknami. It is logical, and it makes sense that the Wlewa would both focus on the Gurahl tribe and be more successful because they rely on caves much more than the Garou. It would have been good to get some version of this in Rise of the Garou rather than just the Aknami died.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">A pile of the Wlewa falling to the Wyrm makes sense, and would probably be a super cool era to game. But dealing with that in addition to everything else sounds like the first domino falling.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I like that this also shows that this isn\u2019t just a two sided war, the Khara are at war with both the Wlewa and the proto-Garou.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">And we have another great ritual, the Great Assumption, but the Wlewa are forced to use other Bastet mages as well and those guys mess with the ritual. This is why you don\u2019t force people to work for you, they will sabotage you when they get the chance and likely when it will hurt the most. And that quickly, at least in War of Rage terms, causes the whole Hamaal system to shatter. That is a big domino.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I am not sure I like the bit about the Wlewa being responsible for the Garou driving many Bastet tribes to the edge of extinction. The Garou should be left with plenty of blame for the modern state of the Fera.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Again we get details of a key part of the Garou\u2019s side of the War, the Ritual of Dark Taking, in this book. Useful, but some of this would have been helpful in the Garou book.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">It would seem that the Wlewa only really took to Eldritch Magic in the latter parts of the war. I still think there should be some Wlewa dabbling in it generations before. At the latest it should be a thing that starts creeping in when they are dealing with their Wyrm fallen kin.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I am confused on how the North American portion of the Wlewa died out.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Mechanics</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Their Yava make sense. Their two totems are good, though I think less powerful than many of the Garou ones from that book.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">There are 20 gifts for the Wlewa, in addition to their breed gifts and general Bastet ones; 11 are re-used. Decisions Well Made seems super on point for the Wlewa and all of them having it makes sense. The mix of Theurge and Philodox gifts is logical and I like basically all the gifts available. This is one of the shortest lists of gifts for any Savage Age Fera though.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">There are 4 rites listed, all of them are interesting and potentially useful to player characters. There is also one fetish and one talens, both interesting.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">5 solid character concepts, I would still like some general ideas of how to distribute stats to help new players or make generating npcs faster.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Given how much is packed in this book this section feels a bit light.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Wlewa in the modern nights</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The idea of the Wlewa and the Garou kind of ending up in cold war is cool and could be a good what if scenario. Maybe something to throw a Garou party into if they are questioning if the Garou nation was right in the War of Rage.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">It could also be cool for a more spy themed campaign.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Appendixes</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">We get a quick rundown of the various other Bastet that can be found in any real number in the Hamaal. Lots of these could be cool options for a pure lands campaign. And we get Yava, form stats and details of what gift list they get in addition to breed and general Bastet so they are easily playable.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Then we get an appendix describing Eldritch Sorcery which is cool, I like how it is somewhat moldable. This could be a scary thing to use even in modern nights for some kind of NPC Fera Sorcerer.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The last appendix is some details on the various animal kin of the Fera in this book. As usual a useful addition.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Overall Thoughts</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">For its length there is a ton of material in this book. It is probably the most space efficient Savage Age book so far. I got to the end of Mantle of leadership without really looking at the page count and thought I must be about to hit the mechanics stuff, nope turned out there is an entire chapter on the War of Rage. And it is important, this book got into a level of specifics that previous Savage Age books seemed hesitant to do. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Eldritch Sorcery stuff is great. The mechanical stuff that is here is good, but I wish there was a little more.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Every Savage Age book seems to make the whole better, building a fuller picture of the era. I think there is now a solid picture of how the War of Rage goes down, and even though it lasts generations there are different cool elements to use at various points in the war for a campaign. At the same time it seems to ebb and flow in intensity, so even if you are doing a game that is taking place during the War of Rage it might never become a major thing.</span></span><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12161-browman","name":"Browman","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"135"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-06-10T17:26:29-07:00","id":1551576,"title":"","content":"<b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Ghost Lineages: the Advanced Players Guide to the Savage Age</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Before getting into this one I should note that I got a free copy of this book.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">This is a thick book, I was not expecting nearly 300 pages. I like given that it is that size, that we are getting some introductory stuff to start out. Going through this book is probably going to take a while since in addition to being like double the size of the previous largest Savage Age book I went through, I feel like there is going to be a lot of time spent looking at other relevant material, like sections of Accounting for the Dead, Changing Breeds, and Rise of the Garou.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">A consolidated list of Gaian mandates is good. And we see some people who haven\u2019t shown up in other books, including the good old Werehyenas now the Ajah-Abah so likely paired with some other hyena like creature from this era as well, also the Kor who I know nothing about though I know there is a Savage Age tribebook for them. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Next we get a timeline, I am very happy to see this. This book did come out nearly 3 years after Rise of the Garou so it is good to see they are being more specific about stuff now. I like that they are willing to say theirs is better than the official one. This timeline is extensive, running a little over 18 pages. While vaguely interesting I don\u2019t really care about the ancient stuff millions of years before the Savage Age. This timeline gives many different points in the Wars of Rage that could be gamed. Personally I think my first campaign will be set around 23,000 BC; so after the conflicts of Granite and Darkness, around the time the Garou Nation is founded. But so many of these bullet points could be the starting point for a campaign. </span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Apis</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">So the Apis already have a bunch of details for this era between the W20 changing breeds stuff and the Accounting for the Dead stuff. But some of that stuff is more geared at \u201cSome the Apis returned\u201d or was very early in the Savage Age development. I am very interested in what will come here. The Apis get 46 pages which is probably the size of Tribebook: Khara once you remove title pages, tables of contents and the like. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The loss of the Apis towards the end of the War of Rage, may be the thing that truly doomed the world. If they were around it would be far easier for the Garou and other Fera to rebuild their numbers.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The most interesting part to me about their really old stuff is the shift of animal kin, The Apis aren\u2019t just moving from closely related species but jumping to entirely different kin. The Rite of Manifold Progression is an awesome name for a mega-rite. The details of this rite are really interesting, along the the reaction of other Fera species.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Apis totems, awesome, hopefully we get some rules for them.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The sidebar about Apis unwittingly starting down the path for weaver drones is cool, and would make a weird NPC for a party to encounter/ deal with.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">There is a lot of extra about how the Apis think and operate. Between this, Accounting for the Dead and Changing Breeds any player should have an excellent idea of the \u201cstandard\u201d Apis. Seeing the world in a series of threes is a cool philosophical lens to give them. It seems like a good reverse engineering from their 3 auspices. This also gives several easy archetypes for players to slot into; each of the sub-roles of the three roles can easily be the start of a character concept.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Chiefdoms are cool, hopefully we get more details on some of them. Accounting for the Dead briefly mentioned different Apis \u201ctribes\u201d but here we start getting some more detail on them, including different non-aurochs animal kin for two of the camps, one of which has two sub-camps.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">It makes sense that there are few Neanderthal Apis or Apis kinfolk. As a progress focused Fera, they would take to Sapiens as soon as they appear. Though a Neanderthal Apis from a forgotten line of kinfolk could be a cool character.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Apis impacting human burial and religious rites makes sense and could easily be an interesting plot line in an Apis focused campaign. The stuff about their colours and symbols is cool and would really help a storyteller describe an Apis and kinfolk community to the players. The details of their Umbral realms are cool, especially the tie to one of them changing considerably over the millenia.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Them being interested in and using Drones is a cool bit, that could lead to some interesting story ideas. Even in modern nights could you have an Apis Drone that has been hidden away by the weaver for some task?</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I love that the Savage Age is frequently giving hints of how various changes in the fates of various Fera could shift the modern nights. A weaver dominated Apis run world could be horrifying.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">We get a few more details on the Last hope mentioned in Changing Breeds, which remains as a plot hook that could be used to bring some Apis into the modern era.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Now we get more details of the tribes or camps of the Apis. It is interesting and gives more options for players, I wonder if we will see any mechanical differences for them. Did some of the Kaunta survive longer than the rest?</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">More details on the Apis involvement in the Wars of Rage, they are the first alliance to get called on triggering a domino effect that spirals the Wars out of control. And we are getting specifics, named elders of various groups. Named caerns. There is plenty here that can be used in character backgrounds or as plot points in a campaign.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Details about their alliances and enemies is useful. Ratkin being their original enemy makes complete sense.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Unlike others, the lack of any Metis for the Apis makes sense. I like that we are now going with each Apis auspice corresponds to a Garou one, it still leaves them with gaps compared the the Garou\u2019s five, but it allows for an all Apis party to have plenty of differences.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">There are another 5 gifts added as well as the auspice expansion. All new. The 3rd rank gift seems redundant with one of the Changing Breeds gifts. All the other ones seems good.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Three new rites are added for the Apis as well. A minor rite, an interesting 4th level rite and an incredibly powerful 6th level rite that an entire campaign would be based around. We also get some more details about how the Grondr become the skull pigs.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">There is a pile of new Talens, including a whole category of talens plants and wyrm corrupted versions. These are super cool and fitting for the Apis, even in campaigns where Apis are not the focus some of these could end up being given as payment across Fera groups.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Three totems as fits a Fera who see three as being the perfect number. All are good with useful bonuses and interesting bans.</span></span><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12161-browman","name":"Browman","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"135"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-06-12T17:44:53-07:00","id":1551643,"title":"","content":"<b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Bastet</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Interesting that they are being covered as one group, since they have never really been organized as such. Describing them as cousins makes sense. We get a quick rundown of some really old big cat species. Bastet generally favouring Sapiens over other forms of human make sense.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Bastet section is even bigger than the Apis section at 51 pages.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The bit about the Ajah-Abah and their becoming the Ajaba is cool. This is the most detail I have seen about the Ajah-Abah so far.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Again we are establishing specifics, in this case about numbers of Fera. It makes complete sense with several more types of Bastet that they are among the most numerous, surpassing even the Canid-shifters at the start of the Savage Age.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I like that we are getting details on Bastet that aren\u2019t Savage Age exclusive. Bubasti magic groupings and Khan alliances with Grondr are both interesting. The idea of hybrid Bastet is cool, both powerful but with interesting limitations.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">It looks like we are going to a page or two dedicated to each Bastet tribe in addition to the general stuff. This will make it much easier to run Khan, Simba and other classic Bastet that survive to the modern era. So the Wlewa and the Simba logically were once one Bastet tribe, I hope we get some detail on what caused the split. The Bubasti also using the Wlewa Eldritch magic makes sense, I would probably have some modern Bubasti still use it, their rules for using other magic have always been a bit clunky. The Swara being the first to figure out the wider War of Rage is happening and name it such is a cool detail.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Overall the non-mechanical portion of this section, like the Apis stuff is great. There are so many details that help build out the world that a campaign lives within.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">We also get more information about how the War of Rage impacts all the Bastet, not just the Wlewa and Khara. It very much seems like if the enemies of the Garou were actually willing to work together, the Garou would have lost.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Their friends and foes are expected, their only friends being the werebats and wereravens may be a sign of why they lost.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">It is good that they list the form stats of all the Bastet here, in case there is any confusion.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Four new gifts, each for a different Bastet tribe. The Wlewa one especially is super cool.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Also four new rites, I particularly like Of Ghosts and Darkness, cool effects also references a good movie and Taste of the Land.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">And four fetishes and a talens, we have a good, if fragile weapon, a tattoo, a stone and a magical rope. All are useful in the right situations. A talens that gets Bastet high is amusing.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">There is a great Khan art piece at the end of this section.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Garou Nation</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">A large section with 63 pages. A good reminder that for much of the era, Garou is similar to Bastet rather than referring to one specific Fera.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The early part of this section reinforces how different and similar the Savage Age Garou are from modern Garou. The modern tribes couldn\u2019t put aside their differences if they were under attack like the Savage Age ones were. Also despite how terrible a price their victory came at, I am not sure they had a choice but to completely destroy the Wlewa and possibly the Khara, others far less so. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">All the Fera contributed to the Wars of Rage happening. The Garou made unreasonable demands, but so did the Apis, the Grondr and several Bastet tribes.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Now we start getting to the various Canid-shifter groups, beginning with the Anupu-Ba-El, who there hasn\u2019t been a ton about since Accounting for the Dead when the Savage Age was still getting its feet under itself. Their focus is clearly on Spirits and the Umbra, and apparently vampires, who I wouldn\u2019t have thought were a problem in this era. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Then we get the Atzu Telal Nahau who might be the most important member of the early Garou Nation since their rite of dark taking is key to breaking the Wlewa and the Hamaal.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I like that the Bilai are so crazy even the other proto-Garou are kind of scared of them.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">So the Kucha get in over their heads, and then get salty when other Fera stop answering their calls. Some Denisovians tapping into Mnesis is a cool idea. What happens if that bloodline becomes kinfolk and Kucha? </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">And we come to the non-Canid members of the Garou Nation, the Saksom Aknami. I still think that the Canid-shifters ensured that there were no others standing beside them at the end of the Wars of Rage.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Then we have the Siberakh, who weirdly seem to be in the best situation of any members of the Garou Nation. That may be what saved the Garou Nation.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Interestingly we also have a piece on the Tengger. I thought they originated further east and north than this says. But that wasn\u2019t based on anything specific. There is enough detail here for them to be used in a game. And the bit about their main Caern being attacked is interesting, I assume it was other Garou, pissed that they haven\u2019t joined.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">When this section starts talking about Fera beyond Canids joining the Garou I wish there were a few more specifics. What Fera was the offer made too?</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Garou not recognizing territorial claims of other Fera makes sense, but is also something that is obviously going to cause conflict with them. But this is the kind of thing where they do that, then get hurt that the other Fera don\u2019t just accept it. Only really having exceptions for the Apis and Corax is interesting and the Apis one won\u2019t last.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The details about various Canid-shifters use of glyphs is good. Being able to include details like that in descriptions can really help </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">We also get some details of the canid view of the umbra. The details of their various umbral realms and how they will splinter in time is really cool. Of course the realms they misuse and break the worst are the ones that belong to the Saksom Aknami. The details of the great mountain in the Siberakh realm are interesting and cool be a cool umbral quest for higher ranked characters to undertake.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Now we get more details on the War of Rage from the Garou perspective. Rise of the Garou already had an extensive write-up on this so I am hoping we get into specifics. I do find it interesting that as the Savage Age has filled out a timeline, that really the modern situation and understanding of Garou is only a small portion of their history. I do like the added detail that the thing that really pushed the War of Rage into gear was that both the Kucha Sakurai and Grondr of the east were killing each other faster than their birth rates; but in typical Fera fashion they both called for allies rather than negotiated. The Wlewa getting involved causing the Garou to basically assume everyone not with them is an enemy is also very on brand for them.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Anupu rarely being the frontline fighters makes sense as the War of Rage is changing the umbra and they are not the best canid fighters. I would think the Anupu are one of the canid groups that comes through the Wars of Rage the worst, as other Fera are far stronger in Africa when everything is said and done. It is interesting that they fought the Grondr and Apis the most, since I would think the African Bastet would have been attacking them.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">It is interesting that the Nahau are one of the closer canids to the Apis homelands, yet are less antagonistic towards them than most of the other proto-Garou. Other than some more bits about the Rite of Dark Taking and its impact on the Nahau and their enemies we don\u2019t get a ton of detail about their actions in the War of Rage.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">And the Bilai take things so far that even the other canids are skeptical of them. Which certainly fits with how several of their descendant tribes operate. They also take heavy casualties and even have to give up territory because they lack the numbers to defend it. They mention some falling to the Wyrm but I am surprised it isn\u2019t higher.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Kucha are based in south Asia and Africa, probably the two worst places to be a canid-shifter in the War of Rage. There is a reason that only the Red Talons really come from these guys. As indicated here, the Garou aren\u2019t the only ones doing dumb things to escalate the situation; the Grondr could solve things without making a public show of it. As suspected, they lost huge amounts of territory and their african and asian branches are completely separated.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Saksom Aknami bit has some cool details about their relationship with various facets of the Wyrm, including the corruptor.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">While I like the Siberakh section, I would have liked some details about them fighting the Khan or the eastern werebears that will go extinct in the War of Shame. I think the information about them being the ones that first found tribal totems for the canids is new, certainly a good detail that could drive a whole campaign.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Some good stuff on the Tengger as well, convincing humans to attack Fera with stone age weapons would be a challenge.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Interesting that the Ratkin show up now, they haven\u2019t been a big thing in most of the Savage Age stuff so far. And shows how different they were, no way modern Ratkin are trying to mediate anything except a murder convention. And the Garou, of course, react badly. The eradication of the ratkin bards was already a canon event, but I don\u2019t think it was specifically said that the Garou did it.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Wailosi are a cool idea, and sound like there are enough of them that they could conceivably have control over a low level caern somewhere.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">As expected, the Garou\u2019s list of allies is short, and their list of enemies extensive.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Now we get to the mechanical section beginning with a basic overview of Garou stats. Then we have 11 new gifts, none of which are reused. And there are ones any Garou can take, along with breed and auspice specific ones. Fangs Bared is super cool. Hunt the Guilty is also cool, but I wonder if it would have been better as a rite. One Pack is really cool for combat. And I like that a few of the gifts are also usable by the right Bastet. Hide Cloak is a crazy gift that could easily get others skeptical about if you are corrupted or not.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Three new rites. All are interesting, some would probably be seen by the Garou\u2019s opponents as evidence that they are corrupted.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Two high level fetishes, the wall is super cool, the unique pelts sound interesting, but the entry appears incomplete.</span></span><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12161-browman","name":"Browman","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"135"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-06-15T18:34:05-07:00","id":1551699,"title":"","content":"<b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Grondr</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Now we come to the Grondr who haven\u2019t gotten covered much since Accounting for the Dead. They get just shy of 40 pages.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Preferring kinfolk with Gnosis is interesting, how common is that in the Savage Age? I assume more common than the incredibly rare ones in the modern era.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I don\u2019t remember the Grondr being so clearly aligned with the Destroyer Wyrm in earlier products. It is interesting that some Fera of this era are trying to split various members of the Triat into parts and claiming to only serve one. A constant trickle of Grondr falling to the Corruptor Wyrm is not going to help them avoid issues with the Garou.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Spiritually active fungus is a nice detail. We already knew that the Apis are some of their strongest allies. Are some Grondr the first Black Spiral Dancers? That sounds terrifying.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The section about the Grondr and Apis working together to control spiritually and physically corrupted plants is excellent. And probably the kind of thing nearly no Garou would understand.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The bit about twin births being more common among Grondr kinfolk is cool. Letting the Werehyenas hunt any of their kind they feel are tainted is a good detail. We haven\u2019t seen a ton on the Werehyenas, so details like this help establish them in the setting more.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The details about how their territory is kept is very useful. Some Garou would probably see modifying the natural environment as Weaver and/or Wyrm taint depending on the changes. Also it may give players hints that they are near a Grondr sounder based on some of the stuff you mention.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Also a description of their normal combat style is useful for a storyteller needing to run a group of Grondr warriors. And the mention of a bane cleansing gift they shared with the Gurahl is great. I don\u2019t think it has been detailed so hopefully it shows up in the gifts section of this book. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The symbols section is also very helpful. Painted and tattoos really help with a visual description and once players understand some of the basics you can give them hints of things without having to draw as much attention to them. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">And we get a quick shot of the Grondr view of the Umbra; their practice of showing up and calling out Fera for stuff is not going to make many friends. The details of the shift of their umbral realm into a modern one is great.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">A small section on the Grondr and Gurahl relations which also touches on the Apis. Reinforcing that if a campaign involves one of them extensively the others should show up sometimes. Garou attacking a fold or sounder might find that members of the other Fera are there. And a Werebear surrounded by a bunch of Wereboars is a very different problem than just the Wereboars.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">A treaty negotiated with a specific group of spirits is very interesting. Especially since other Fera are also involved and it has some unintended consequences. Acid Flux is a cool idea, and would make a great plotline in a campaign, even a non-Grondr one that has to deal with a Wyld corrupted Grondr in their territory and the fallout from that.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">More detail on the various \u201ctribes\u201d of Grondr, this type of detail is very useful in the Savage Age where regional differences can help make things more interesting. There was an American tribe as well, mentioned in Accounting for the Dead, it would have been good to have a few lines on. I also like the detail that much of their organization is matriarchies. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">And now their side of the War of Rage, probably the most interesting note in that the Wereboars fight especially hard in the \u201cNear East\u201d. Of course the Garou believe that no other Fera could know things the Garou don\u2019t. The details about their totem, aligned spirits and the Wyrm is excellent. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The creation of the Skull Pigs now makes a lot more sense than Accounting for the Dead\u2019s version. It would be cool if a group of Wyld corrupted Grondr also survived.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Grondr had a long list of allies, and an equally long list of enemies. Again we hear about the Ajah-Abah. A tribebook on them would be cool.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Now we get to the new mechanical stuff for the Grondr. 7 new gifts, several tied to the Acid Flux Wyld tainted Grondr mentioned earlier. Wyld poop is a very random thing to have an NPC do. One reused rite that is amusing. One fetish and 3 talens; the fetish is powerful but dangerous, the talens interesting and potentially the source of adventures. The three totems are useful.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Gurahl</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Gurahl are a conservative Fera, one that we have only really heard about one tribe of, the one that joined the Garou Nation. But the Gurahl have always featured heavily in the official write-ups about the War of Rage. Most of them will be driven into hibernation and at some point the Asian tribe driven to extinction. We already knew the stone eaters preferred neanderthals now we learn that they are preferred by the Gurahl across tribes. This suggests that some of the elders that sleep for millenia and return in the modern era are neanderthal rather than sapiens.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">A first Great Council implies at least a second, if not more.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I am not sure exactly what is being gained by adding another, dead, tribe of Gurahl.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The phrasing of the Dragon Slayers section is odd, the information is interesting and shows the damage that the Fera fighting among themselves is causing. But it is written as if the events are actively happening; also the War of Rage lasts for millenia, how long are the Gurahl preparing and how long does an individual Gurahl live?</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">We get some details about the Gurahl social structure, which sounds somewhat different than modern. The sacrifice and rebirth stuff is very interesting. The Lifework is a cool expansion of their love of craft in modern times.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">There is a lot of extra stuff here that will make running a non-Stone Eater Gurahl much easier in the Savage Age. How they form groups, their relationships with kinfolk and many other details that will round out a story.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The details of their annual social gatherings are super cool. These events should be used as parts of any Gurahl focused campaign. Information about the wider world can be provided, potential storylines started, allies, or rivals, made. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Gurahl and Ratkin not liking each other makes sense, though my guess is the Ratkin have literally orders of magnitude more of their kind and can just overwhelm a Gurahl with numbers.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Neanderthals turning to the Wyrm for survival is a cool detail that won\u2019t end well.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Now we get Savage Age names of the modern Gurahl tribes which is a nice detail. And in these short tribe write-ups we get plenty of story hooks. It is something I think the Savage Age has done well, that there is plenty going on that doesn\u2019t require the War of Rage to dominate (or even appear in) a campaign. It sounds like a significant storyline could be done on the nightmare kings. Ice Stalkers being the most liberal of the tribes is interesting. There being a break off group of Stone Eaters that don\u2019t join the Garou Nation is interesting. I would think they eventually get absorbed into the other tribes, probably mostly the Mountain Guardians.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The rest of the Gurahl not being cool with a bunch of what the Stone Eaters do with the Garou Nation makes sense. The Rogijo are a good idea, and opens the option for a player character. And the Garou basically only seeing the world in black and white forces the Gurahl against them.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The idea that the Gurahl did some kind of ritual to prevent the stone eaters from passing on certain things to the Garou is cool. I like that the Savage Age has a bunch of different groups doing incredibly powerful rites and rituals.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Other Gurahl being the ones to put down the last of the Stone Eaters is an interesting detail along with the Death Bear\u2019s response. Even in a non-Gurahl campaign, having that happen out of nowhere and shutting off Umbra access during a key moment is the kind of thing players will remember. During that time, are any Fera in the Umbra stuck on that side? Or are they forced out?</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Now we get into the mechanical side of the Gurahl, I have always thought their rotation through the various auspices is a cool idea. I like how here the general time when they transition from one to the next is given.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Eight new gifts including one Stone Eater specific gift. Purge the Soul is a great gift and makes complete sense for the Gurahl. The reincarnation gift is also really cool. Three new rites, all are super cool. Two fetishes and a talens, the idea of a fetish being a powerful \u201ctrue name\u201d is very interesting, the others are good. Four different totems, again it is good to see totems for all sorts of different Fera.</span></span><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12161-browman","name":"Browman","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"135"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-06-16T17:22:43-07:00","id":1551729,"title":"","content":"<b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Appendixes</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">We get a couple of pages on the idea of gods in the stone age, which is very helpful for people running campaigns where mortals are involved a bunch. I like that every big Savage Age book has some details on the time period to help with gaming in it. Then we get a list of what Fera are around in different eras; interestingly the Ananasi don\u2019t appear on this list except maybe under \u201cInsect Shifters\u201d in the oldest era.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Overall Thoughts</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">This book is packed with information. Significant expansion in detail on all the Fera covered. In most cases basically the equivalent of getting a tribebook on each of them. One of the useful things is different Fera getting specific totems for them. As always there is great art.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">This book reinforces that the Garou won the War(s) of Rage because they were able to work together and their enemies weren\u2019t. If the Apis, Grondr, Wlewa and their Hamaal allies, Ajah-Abah, Khara, other Bastet and Gurahl had all actually worked together, the Garou were doomed.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I like that this book covered a bunch of Fera that hadn\u2019t previously had a lot of detail in other Savage Age books. Granted I haven\u2019t read ever single Savage Age product published prior to this, but I doubt the various Tribebooks, comics, short story collections and a sourcebook mostly on items really dive into the Gurahl, or Bastet. Again this book builds on everything that has come before and will add value to almost any Savage Age campaign. It is not easy to make that the case with book after book. Even if you were doing say a Mokole focused campaign, the information about other Fera you will encounter would be useful.</span></span><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12161-browman","name":"Browman","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"135"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-06-17T20:11:20-07:00","id":1551749,"title":"","content":"<b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Ancestral Memories: The Advanced Player's Guide to the Savage Age Vol 2</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">First I should note that I got a free copy of this book. This book covers 5 Fera that we haven\u2019t seen a ton from in other Savage Age products I have seen so far. I know there is a Corax book that I need to pick up at some point, but honestly the pre-Ice and Stone Ages stuff doesn\u2019t really interest me. The other four, Mokole, Nagah, Ratkin and Rokea get mentioned in places but there hasn\u2019t been a ton of detail. I suspect that will in some ways make this different from Ghost Lineages since most of those Fera had at least some detail in previous books. There are two appendices at the end covering more general Savage Age stuff. This book is also much shorter than Ghost Lineages.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Gurahl short story at the start is great, kind of surprising it is a Gurahl story when they aren\u2019t a focus of this book. Still Gurahl are great.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Corax</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I have always found the Corax interesting, though I think they can be difficult to include as a player character. They make great NPCs, and are super useful for getting information to the PCs that may otherwise be difficult for them to know. They can be a great way to introduce the concept of other Fera to players who have only dealt with werewolves.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">So the Corax are now the last member of a larger grouping of Fera called the Rapax that seem kind of like the Bastet in that it is a grouping of similar types of Fera. Are we going to see more about these Corax Memnae? That would be cool.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Overall 33 pages on the Corax including several particularly good pieces of art. There are several pages of pre-pre-history for the Corax, it is well written but I don\u2019t really care about ancient Mokole kingdoms from 10s of millions of years ago. The Mind-of-Thought-and-Memory is a cool idea. Corax with Mnesis, I would like to know more; though it would seem that ability dies out in the Savage Age. Still that would be a cool Corax to use as an NPC in the modern era. Corax helping to shape the Umbral realms of the dead is cool.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The different philosophies of Corax are cool, and having both rooted and rootless Corax can be useful for a campaign.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I knew real life ravens were smart, I didn\u2019t realize they were as smart as the information in this book shows they are. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Using slight modifications to feathers to store information is cool. Their reasons for liking trees and disliking fire are logical and interesting. The details of their Umbral realms are good; it would have been cool to have a nod towards Yggdrasil with their world tree.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The three camps are all interesting and could each be interesting characters. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Corax part on the War of Rage should be interesting, they are one of the few Fera that get along with nearly everyone else. At the same time, knowledge is power, and few in the War of Rage want to share power. I suspect that most of the Corax of the era are killed and given how Corax create more of themselves their population growth is very slow, it will take generations to recover from major losses. The Wlewa being the ones who really target the Corax makes complete sense.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The section on Corax use of language is really good, and can help give a Savage Age game a certain feel.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The allies section gives us a little bit on the Werebats of the Savage Age, who I know have their own book but are not mentioned often elsewhere. I am interested in learning why they change their name. In the enemies portion there is mention of the Wlewa wiping out another Fera, when did this happen, I don\u2019t remember it from the Ghost Lineages timeline. I really like the idea that the Rokea hate the Corax and the Corax don\u2019t know why.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The basic Corax mechanical stuff is good. I like that an entire Garou Auspice and Tribe gift lists are suggested as possible options to consider, that significantly expands the options available to Corax which would be important if you have 2 or more in a group, especially an all Corax campaign. 16 Gifts, including ones that only some Corax can take. Phoenix Raven is particularly cool. The combat gifts give some interesting options.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Two interesting and potentially useful rites. Three fetishes and 2 talens all useful and fitting for Corax.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">This section ends with another great piece of art.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Mokole</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I am somewhat skeptical about all the changes the Savage Age has made to Mokole ancient history. Let\u2019s see if this section changes that. 39 pages for the Mokole. Another awesome art piece. I still dislike that the Mokole seem to have wildly gone against the will of Gaia, even more so than other Fera and seemingly suffered few consequences. Again the stuff about truly ancient times is well written but I just don\u2019t care about that era.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I find it interesting that as we get to the Savage Age section it says they are now only werecrocodiles. In the modern era, there are other reptile Mokole, including of course Komodo Dragons. I am not sure how I feel about the Mokole already seeming to be withdrawing from the wider world in the Savage Age. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The details about how the Mokole organize are useful, but not really anything new. Experimentation on homids is interesting, hopefully we get more about that.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I am slightly confused about why there appear to be no Mokole in the pure lands; the gators and crocodiles in Florida and nearby areas have been there a long time. The bit about colours is an interesting detail. It is always good to get details on how different Fera view and use the Umbra.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">We have a mix of existing and new streams, which I think is good. I like that they aren\u2019t just regional groups, they have different organizational styles and alliances and rivals. It makes things more interesting, gives more replayability to the Mokole and could be a source of conflict if members of one stream visit another.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">And we get more detail on the various animal-kin types of Mokole. The Ao go extinct at some point, likely during the War of Rage. The Kratii are interesting, the idea that the Nagah and Mokole at one point were one Fera is interesting. I suspect the Nagah helped the Rokea finish them off.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">What is this about reawakened old ones? Can the Mokole slumber like the Gurahl?</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Mokole often being most concerned with preventing others from accessing Mnesis is interesting and could lead to some interesting adventures, or an excuse to have some Mokole NPCs show up somewhere unexpected. I was hoping the bit mentioned in ghost lineages about Denisovan bloodlines with a form of Mnesis would show up in this section, so I am glad to see it. Interestingly it is expanded to a bunch of different Fera and the Mokole don\u2019t seem to realize that list includes the Grondr. We also get some idea of when this happens as the Khara are apparently \u201cwiped out\u201d presumably the european variety, since the Mokole don\u2019t seem to have a presence in the Americas. That would mean this happens after 10,000 BC, which is slightly odd given the Denisovans should be gone by then, unless the ability transferred to Sapiens lines that mixed with them. The Ajah-Abah are listed here as the Ajah and the Abah, so perhaps at this point have different types of Hyena kinfolk and are different \u201ctribes\u201d. Of course the immediate explanation the Kucha have is that the Mokole are wyrmspawn.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Overall the War of Rage stuff is good but fairly expected. I wish there was a bit more about Africa with the Kucha there. Their list of allies and enemies is mostly what you would expect.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Mechanically Mokole are somewhat complicated, with their many different auspices and different Varnas. Getting a bunch of glyphs in this section is cool. Every Auspice having two different Garou lists to potentially draw from is a huge increase in potential options. 6 generic gifts, 4 Rites, 1 fetish, and 2 talens add a bunch of new options. Mnesis Venatus is a really cool gift, though all of them are interesting. The rite that allows for Mokole Metis to actually be born is a cool, twisted rite that could cause a ton of drama.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Sorrow Stones are really cool, you would have to be very careful using them. Both talens are cool. </span></span><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12161-browman","name":"Browman","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"135"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-06-20T19:18:13-07:00","id":1551794,"title":"","content":"<b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Nagah</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">A 35 page section on the Nagah. They have always been an interesting Fera that is difficult to use in a modern campaign due to being hidden from all the others unless you are running a pure Nagah group. Assuming they operate more openly in the Savage Age you could actually use one in a multi-Fera campaign.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The most interesting part of the early history stuff is the Nagah once being considered the experts on the Umbra.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Nagah conflict with various asian Bastet, probably mainly Khan, is logical and could be very interesting to game. Their more controlling nature over their kinfolk and even regular humans is different in a good way from many other Fera. As with most other Fera we get details of their organization, which is different than when they go into hiding during the War of Rage. Given their dependence on water, organizing based around river systems makes sense. Nagah being healers is interesting, they don\u2019t have a ton of overlap with the Gurahl so they won\u2019t really get in each other\u2019s way.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The European Nagah not needing water to access the umbra is interesting. And we get a specific named Nagah which is rare in this type of Savage Age book. Nagah valuing precious stones is also a nice detail, having information like this makes it easier to describe things in different ways for different groups.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Like the other Fera in this book we see information about the Nagah\u2019s view and use of the Umbra, also very helpful.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">It is good to see a version of the Nagah\u2019s involvement with starting the War of Rage included. Them often being busy fighting Ratkin is an interesting way to keep them out of the main conflict. But the Nagah stabbing everyone from the shadows escalates things rather than calms them down, you can\u2019t half ass killing your way to peace. All the stuff about them in the War of Rage make sense, and is well written. Again the Garou win, because their many enemies can\u2019t work together.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I find it interesting that the Nagah are worried about falling to the Wyrm. And things have gone badly enough that at least one regional group of them are entirely gone. We do get some details about Wyrm corrupted Nagah though which is cool. There are also details about the formation of the Sesha which will lead the modern version of the Fera.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Their list of friends is short, and even those are shaky at best. Their two main enemies probably the most populous Fera.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">As usual we get a basic overview of the mechanics of Nagah, and like other Fera in this book there are suggestions for Garou lists to consider for different auspices. 7 general gifts, 2 rites, 1 fetish, 4 talens and 3 mutually exclusive merits that each unlock a set of 5 gifts.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The gifts, rites and fetish are all decent, nothing stands out as particularly awesome or bad. The talens being incenses that has different effects on Nagah and non-Nagah is really cool, I like it when Fera have talens, fetishes, rites and the like that do or use things the Garou would never care about. The merits and the gift lists they unlock are probably the best part of this section.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Ratkin</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Oh the crazy skaven, I mean Ratkin. Like the Nagah they can be hard to use in modern nights, though Garou having to deal with a nest of these can be a nasty problem to deal with. From what little I have seen on Savage Age Ratkin they aren\u2019t quite as insane as in the modern era, so perhaps could join a mixed Fera party. This section is 53 pages, that gives them easily the largest section so far, larger than the first Savage Age tribebook.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">It is interesting that the Ratkin are not worldwide at this point, and aren\u2019t in significant parts of europe. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Banestorm is a cool idea, especially with it leading to the first Formori; very skaven coded thing. This seems like a really cool era to game, even if you aren\u2019t playing Ratkin. The impacts on the gauntlet from this are also nice. Getting judged by the Nagah and Mokole for a 65 million year old deal that they don\u2019t remember seems on brand for all involved. This whole situation also messing with an Ajah-Abah rite is an extra cherry on top. Speaking of those werehyenias, they have been mentioned in a bunch of places but have never gotten their own time in the limelight, hopefully that changes soon. Different Fera pulling off powerful rites throughout the Savage Age is a good thing, they can make an excellent backdrop for a campaign to be happening during. An Ajah-Abah campaign hunting Ratkin would make a great campaign.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">And it looks like the Savage Age is officially pushing aside the Ananasi as the ones responsible for the Neaderthal cull in their version of events. At this point I doubt the Ananasi even exist in the Savage Age version of the world.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Rats of different colours favouring different roles I think is new. A befits a Wyld aligned Fera, their organization is rather chaotic. Calling their pack sized groups Mischiefs is good.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Losing the Bards will break the Ratkin entirely. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Blood memory sounds like a slightly different version of Mnesis. What happens to a Crinos-born Ratkin if they aren\u2019t exposed to the Birthing Plague?</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Detailing the various ways the Ratkin work to keep human populations under control is good. These can make for good early tasks for a new party to do.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Symbols and colours importance to any Fera is good information to have and helps allow a storyteller to build a description of their caerns or other areas of control.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">As usual we also get information about the Ratkin and the Umbra, honestly I would have thought this section would be bigger given that the Ratkin flee to the Umbra for millenia.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Ratkin being somewhat mercenary in the early millenia of the War of Rage is cool and could lead to some interesting places. An entirely disposable mercenary Mischief of Ratkin could be a surprise to a party who thinks they have all their enemy\u2019s tactics on lock. We also get a detailed look at the Garou wiping out the Bards, I think the most detailed version I have read.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Ratkin viewing the Gurahl as allies is interesting, as I doubt the Gurahl feel the same. Their list of enemies is nearly everyone.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Mechanically Ratkin have a lot going on, they have a ton of different auspices, each with multiple inbuilt advantages and disadvantages. 5 new gifts, some restricted to certain auspices, 1 new rite, 4 fetishes and 3 talens.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The new gifts are all good, the one granting a new form especially. The rite effectively grants a temporary totem to the group and all 3 options are potentially useful. All the fetishes make sense for Ratkin, the portable umbra tunnel should have any players dealing with Ratkin very paranoid. The talens are all cool and useful. 4 Totems, all make sense for the Ratkin.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The other mechanical stuff about blood memory Warpspasms and Wyld Minds really help set a Ratkin game apart from other Fera.</span></span><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12161-browman","name":"Browman","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"135"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-06-21T17:00:34-07:00","id":1551809,"title":"","content":"<b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Rokea</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">And now we get to the Rokea, who even for Fera often feel like White Wolf didn\u2019t actually want them to be playable. Their section is 32 pages long.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">We get a bunch of details on how they live and their use of islands in the ocean, and presumably major seas like the mediterranean give a situation where they can encounter other Fera. Rokea river sharks causing conflict with other Fera is also interesting, presumably they would go to Un-sea more often as well.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The information about how Rokea communicate is interesting.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">As usual we get some details of how the Rokea interact with the Umbra.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Rokea fighting mostly Nagah and Mokole makes sense. It doesn\u2019t seem like either side wins. The information about the flooding of Doggerland is the most interesting part of this section. When in doubt and the Garou don\u2019t understand they assume Wyrm corruption and usually make everything worse.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Kor come back in the Savage Age? That is new, who are they fighting? Their enemies list is expected.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Mechanically it is good that homid Rokea are not literally unique snowflake characters. As always it is good to get an overall mechanical overview for the Rokea. Again we get suggestions of Garou gift lists for each of the Rokea auspices. 9 new gifts, 2 rites, 2 fetishes, 5 talens and 3 breed background merit/gift lists.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Most of the gifts are interesting, nearly all of them are directly combat related, and flying air shark is a silly gift. The 5th level rite is a very cool one that is a campaign centerpiece event. Both fetishes are weapons. The coolest talens are the non-combat ones. I think the merit accessed gift lists are more interesting than the generic ones. Overall this mechanical section seemed heavily combat focused.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">One of my concerns with this section is I still don\u2019t really see how the Rokea can be used very much in most campaigns. </span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Appendices</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">These are nearly identical to the appendices in Ghost Lineages, so there isn\u2019t much to say about them. It is only a handful of pages, but still feels odd.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Overall Thoughts</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The art in all the Savage Age books is excellent, but the art in this book is on another level.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">One thing I have noticed, the weird mostly empty pages are largely gone in recent books, which suggests better book planning.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I don\u2019t really care about the pre-ice/stone age stuff so a significant portion of the histories of most of the Fera in this book was on stuff I am not interested in.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">One of the best things about this book is that every mechanical section has suggestions for Garou lists to consider letting players take gifts from.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Overall I don\u2019t think this book is as worthwhile as Ghost Lineages, it is over 25% shorter, and has a bunch of pre-Savage Age content. I think that moves it from Ghost Lineages excellent value to simply being good value. I do really like that the Savage Age team is still iterating on what they are doing with their books; I have already mentioned several times one of the best examples of this, every Fera in this book has ideas for other lists that gifts could potentially be drawn from. Also the different breed merit/gift lists is a cool idea that could maybe be ported over to other Fera.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">101 Savage Age Kinfolk</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Savage Age Kinfolk is a small and cheap book that has 109 Kinfolk listed. There are no actual stats, just a paragraph or sometimes two about them. There are a few pieces of art, probably more than you would expect for a book this cheaply priced. First we get a pile of Garou Kinfolk, 10 Anupu, 9 Nahau, 9 Bilai, 9 Kucha, 9 Saksom Ankami, and 9 Siberakh. Then we get to the \u201cindependents\u201d, 6 Ajah-Abah, 10 Apis, 9 Grondr, 1 Kor, 2 Naghaluu, and 2 Urkama. The Bastet with 6 Ceilican, 9 Khara, and 9 Wlewa. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Some of these write-ups are more immediately useful than others, especially the ones that describe the kinfolk in some detail. I do like that not all of them are human kinfolk. And that it is at least suggested that some of them have abilities that aren\u2019t completely natural.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I think this book would also be super useful for someone who wasn\u2019t really sure what type of character they wanted to play. A quick read of a few sections and hopefully one of the listed kinfolk gives some ideas as most of these could be the starting point of a more fleshed out character.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">This a super-useful book, it gives lots of great ideas for how you can seed the communities that the players encounter with interesting kinfolk.</span></span><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12161-browman","name":"Browman","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"135"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-06-22T17:53:17-07:00","id":1551823,"title":"","content":"<b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Tribebook: Urkama</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">This is the first book I wasn\u2019t able to get as part of a bundle. Average length for a Savage Age Tribebook, and for a Fera that technically exists in the official lore, but really hasn\u2019t had much written about it.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The first short story is excellent, it conveys a lot about how the Urkama view things. The second one also helps define them, and must be relatively late in the War of Rage if he is dealing with the Garou.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Tribal Background</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Urkamas Gaian Mandate is super interesting and a good example of how the Fera are dealing with things that aren\u2019t just in the \u201creal\u201d world.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The whole idea of Bat once being a servant of the uncorrupted Wyrm and escaping being corrupted, at least for a time, is a good plot idea that already existed within Werewolf, I am glad it has been kept.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Other Fera not trusting the Urkama is very on brand with how Fera strongly dislike anything they can\u2019t immediately understand and tendencies to assume the worst.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Caches of weird and/or interesting fetishes is intriguing, hopefully that shows up in the mechanical section.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Garou choosing to target them towards the end of the War of Rage makes sense, some Garou are going to want to constantly find a new threat to fight and prove themselves against. Also it would be cool to get some more details about the various Imperial Moots, since I assume it wasn\u2019t a regularly scheduled thing.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">And the Garou, led by the Bilai, mess with what they don\u2019t understand and, as usual, never considered the second and third order implications of what they did. You would think at some point the Garou would realize that just because they didn\u2019t understand, didn\u2019t mean there wasn\u2019t something to understand and that messing with it without understanding would bite them in the ass.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Bat starting to crack as early as the end of the War of Rage gives thousands of years for that to play out.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I like how the Urkama are tied into the American ones becoming the Camazotz.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Bat getting directly involved with trying to get a bunch of Garou Totems to back off is an excellent detail. The things the Urkama were keeping at bay allying with the Wyrm both makes sense and helps explain how the Wyrm got both more powerful and more broken after the War of Rage.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I always like seeing Litanies for various Fera.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Secrets Beyond Sight</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I like that we are getting some details about what it is that the Urkama fight at the edges of reality.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Also their need to manipulate others to either attack stuff or avoid stuff could be a good way to include an Urkama in a campaign, have a high ranked Urkama occasionally tell the player character stuff that needs to be massaged into a different place.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Of course other Fera don\u2019t like the Urkama manipulating them, especially into situations where they lose people.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Nightmare Gods</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Given that I think we were just talking about these guys in the last super short chapter, it organizationally feels odd to split them.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I like how we get several different Fera\u2019s ideas on where the Nightmare Gods come from. The rite of the hollow sound doesn\u2019t sound like a good thing, but also the kind of thing that some Garou would be very willing to use. Hopefully we see both versions of this rite.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Blighter of Souls</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Now we get details on a specific problem spirit the Urkama have dealt with, which I think is excellent, early Savage Age products were often hesitant to pin anything down. Things need to be pinned down so there are some parameters for storytellers to work from.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">We specifically have Urkama called shamans, hopefully there is more about that.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The details about fighting this infection would make for an excellent campaign. The Urkama fighting in places the other Fera are unaware of and those Fera thinking the Urkama mostly bailed on them is a good detail. My one confusion with this is was this mostly contained to one region? Or pockets across reality, linked in ways that make no sense to a physical mind? I would have liked it if at least 1 place was called out.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Who They Were</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Some Urkama never making the cut for the deeper mysteries feels very cult like, but also gives them useful cutouts who legitimately don\u2019t know about the stuff they have going on.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Semi-regular umbral gatherings are cool. I wonder if so many of their leadership being regularly in some of the weirdest, most distant parts of reality actually hurt them long term.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Being relatively isolationist among their own kind is a bit odd to me, most bats live in huge colonies, I would assume the Urkama are the same.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Urkama performing their rituals on very small children is an interesting distinction from most other Fera.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Their relations with other Fera are what you would expect. Their distribution of kin feels odd, earlier it seemed like many Urkama were loners, now it seems like they hang in groups.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Urkama in modern nights is interesting, this could be tied into the ideas from the Wlewa book for if they survived. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The blood song and its effects on Urkama and the future Camazotz.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Breath of Life</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Apterous form doesn\u2019t have wings but has a penalty to athletics and dodge while not flying? The other core mechanical stuff seems fine.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">31 Gifts listed, a mix of re-used and new. With the 28 gifts the Camazotz have in W20 changing breeds, most of which would still make sense for the Urkama, you have a decent number of options.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">We start off strong with Anchor the Soul, which seems like a nearly mandatory gift for any Umbral focused Urkama. No escape is also cool. The level one gifts give many good options for several different character ideas. Luna\u2019s Luck is probably my favorite of the level 2 gifts. Pathways through Darkness is a classic rpg idea, and Many Wings Form is cool, all the level 3 gifts are useful with different builds. All 4 level 4 gifts seem very on brand for the Urkama but I would have liked at least one unique level 4 gift. All but one of the level 5 gifts are new, and they are all very cool, Seek the Soul\u2019s Lament seems very important to their Gaian mandate.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Now we have 5 rites, which might be the most in any Savage Age Tribebook. It makes sense that they would have some unique rites though. Mind hardening, den realms, forcibly adjusting spirits, emotion deadening; all of them are cool, though I think the mechanics of the second 5th level rite are a bit confusing. I think it needs 40 gnosis in total, requiring 1 character to generate 40 gnosis is impossible.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Two fetishes, no talens interestingly; both fetishes are useful. We also get 4 interesting totems. All could be good for a player group or npcs.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">And some details on how Urkama determine renown. Always good to see that spelled out. And we get 5 character concepts, I like these better when they have ideas for how to build that character. It helps new players get going or storytellers build quick NPCs.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Appendixes</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The first section is a bunch of information about the Umbra, umbral travel, and a few umbral realms, very important to the Urkama and good to get some additional details on that.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Next we get details on blood magic, the magic section of the Wlewa book was super cool, I have high hopes for this. 5 rites, all are interesting and rather dark. All 5 mostly combat related gifts. This stuff could be be very useful for a modern game, have a mortal wyrm-tainted shaman or even some Black Spiral Dancers or other corrupted Fera with access to this stuff. A new, unexpected problem for the players to deal with.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Last we have an appendix on actual bats, these sections are always useful to help colour player interactions with this type of Fera.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Overall Thoughts</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I like the level of detail we are getting about specific things in this tribebook.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I don\u2019t like the art in this book as much as that of nearly all the other Savage Age books, that isn\u2019t to say it is bad, it just isn\u2019t as good as the others.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Because of their Gaian mandate, the Urkama can end up being reasonably useful even in a campaign that doesn\u2019t star them. Player characters can be diverted to dealing with a problem they found, or can be seeking some fetishes or talens the Urkama have hidden away somewhere.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I think this book gives enough information that you could run an Urkama campaign largely using just this book. I do worry that unless you are dealing with corrupted Urkama with access to blood magic if there would be enough variety of gifts, but that is a problem basically all the Fera that aren\u2019t Werewolves face on some level.</span></span><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12161-browman","name":"Browman","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"135"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-06-24T17:41:31-07:00","id":1551846,"title":"","content":"<b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Overall Savage Age thoughts</span></span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">So we have reached the end of the Savage Age products I own. I know there are bunch of other books they have done that I don\u2019t have yet, like a few other tribebooks, a fiction anthology and a few one shot adventure books. But I have all the main books that cover the Savage Age.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">First, the art is almost universally great. They really help build the feel of the setting, even official books often don\u2019t have this much high quality art.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Second, the Savage Age does both a really good job of showing the big picture of the time period, leading up to and throughout the War of Rage. The Fera created or expanded on for the Savage Age are really cool and generally well fleshed out. The ideas for several different Canid-shifters eventually becoming the Garou and the early Garou Nation having a Gurahl component are amazing.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">It also does a good job at the player level mechanically, detailing exactly how to build all sorts of Fera characters, with more gifts, rites, fetishes, talens, and totems. Many of those things are useful even outside a Savage Age campaign. Especially if you are wanting to use a Fera that never got a lot of love from the official books.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Where I think the Savage Age struggles is giving storytellers a more middle level picture. What I mean by that is details on caerns/septs/folds/sounders/chiefdoms/sebios and various NPC characters. The type of stuff you would find in books like Rage across the Amazon. I 100% get that picking a specific time/place with the Savage Age would be hard, since it covers thousands of years and things change significantly over that time. Entire species of Fera go extinct or others think they do. And the Savage Age has gotten better over time about giving specifics. But it is a significant hurdle for storytellers who want to set an entire campaign in the Savage Age without having an area to hang that story on. And I don\u2019t know if I would have prioritized a Rage Across the Savage Age (or specific location Rage Across _____) over any of the big books it would have replaced. But I think now is the time to add something like that.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">But that doesn\u2019t make the Savage Age bad by any stretch, just that getting a Rage Across ___ book would be the icing on an already good cake.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">And the Savage Age does a really good job of building up, each book makes the whole project better with Tribebooks, Savage Age Volumes and Advanced Players Guides all adding details to the larger setting.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">I think anyone with even a little bit of interest in the time period or who wants extra ideas for some less focused on parts of Werewolf would benefit from getting at least the Savage Age Intro Bundle.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Also it feels like I have kind of been ranting into the wind with writing all of this. If anyone is still paying attention, what did you think of the Savage Age? What did you find most useful from it?</span></span><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83e\udd70","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12161-browman","name":"Browman","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"135"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}